WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.566 --> 00:04.636 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:04.736 --> 00:05.276 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:05.576 --> 00:08.966 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:08.966 --> 00:11.156 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:11.206 --> 00:12.396 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:12.796 --> 00:14.776 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:15.886 --> 00:18.886 align:middle
Today the Federal Open Market
Committee kept interest rates

00:18.956 --> 00:21.686 align:middle
near zero and maintained
our asset purchases.

00:22.446 --> 00:24.796 align:middle
These measures, along
with our strong guidance

00:24.796 --> 00:26.826 align:middle
on interest rates and
on our balance sheet,

00:27.406 --> 00:29.736 align:middle
will ensure that monetary
policy will continue

00:29.736 --> 00:32.376 align:middle
to deliver powerful
support to the economy

00:32.376 --> 00:33.926 align:middle
until the recovery is complete.

00:35.316 --> 00:36.706 align:middle
Widespread vaccinations,

00:36.906 --> 00:39.396 align:middle
along with unprecedented
fiscal policy actions,

00:39.576 --> 00:42.276 align:middle
are also providing strong
support to the recovery.

00:43.386 --> 00:46.096 align:middle
Indicators of economic activity
and employment have continued

00:46.096 --> 00:49.916 align:middle
to strengthen, and real GDP
this year appears to be on track

00:49.916 --> 00:53.026 align:middle
to post its fastest rate
of increase in decades.

00:54.126 --> 00:56.296 align:middle
Much of this rapid growth
reflects the continued

00:56.296 --> 00:58.736 align:middle
bounceback in activity
from depressed levels.

00:59.956 --> 01:01.906 align:middle
The sectors most
adversely affected

01:01.906 --> 01:04.806 align:middle
by the pandemic remain weak
but have shown improvement.

01:05.996 --> 01:08.576 align:middle
Household spending is
rising at a rapid pace,

01:09.016 --> 01:12.516 align:middle
boosted by the ongoing reopening
of the economy, fiscal support,

01:12.906 --> 01:14.626 align:middle
and accommodative
financial conditions.

01:15.566 --> 01:16.896 align:middle
The housing sector is strong,

01:17.246 --> 01:19.986 align:middle
and business investment is
increasing at a solid pace.

01:20.526 --> 01:24.146 align:middle
In some industries, near-term
supply constraints are

01:24.146 --> 01:25.166 align:middle
restraining activity.

01:26.036 --> 01:27.996 align:middle
Forecasts from FOMC participants

01:27.996 --> 01:30.376 align:middle
for economic growth this
year have been revised

01:30.376 --> 01:33.506 align:middle
up since our March Summary
of Economic Projections.

01:34.516 --> 01:36.356 align:middle
Even so, the recovery
is incomplete,

01:36.546 --> 01:38.736 align:middle
and risks to the
economic outlook remain.

01:40.996 --> 01:42.876 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

01:43.126 --> 01:45.796 align:middle
conditions in the labor market
have continued to improve,

01:46.476 --> 01:48.846 align:middle
although the pace of
improvement has been uneven.

01:50.876 --> 01:54.426 align:middle
Employment rose 419,000 per
month, on average, in April

01:54.426 --> 01:57.726 align:middle
and May, with the leisure and
hospitality sector continuing

01:57.726 --> 01:59.186 align:middle
to post notable gains.

02:00.316 --> 02:02.196 align:middle
Employment in this
sector and the economy

02:02.196 --> 02:05.216 align:middle
as a whole remains well
below pre-pandemic levels.

02:06.506 --> 02:10.036 align:middle
The unemployment rate remained
elevated in May at 5.8 percent,

02:10.476 --> 02:13.036 align:middle
and this figure understates
the shortfall in employment,

02:13.726 --> 02:17.296 align:middle
particularly as participation in
the labor market has not moved

02:17.296 --> 02:19.686 align:middle
up from the low rates
that have prevailed

02:19.686 --> 02:20.986 align:middle
for most of the past year.

02:22.386 --> 02:25.566 align:middle
Factors related to the pandemic,
such as caregiving needs,

02:25.566 --> 02:29.616 align:middle
ongoing fears of the virus, and
unemployment insurance payments,

02:30.026 --> 02:31.976 align:middle
appear to be weighing
on employment growth.

02:32.906 --> 02:36.016 align:middle
These factors should wane in
coming months against a backdrop

02:36.016 --> 02:38.206 align:middle
of rising vaccinations, leading

02:38.206 --> 02:40.026 align:middle
to more rapid gains
in employment.

02:41.436 --> 02:44.506 align:middle
Looking ahead, FOMC participants
project the labor market

02:44.506 --> 02:47.196 align:middle
to continue to improve,
with the median projection

02:47.196 --> 02:50.186 align:middle
for the unemployment rate
standing at 4.5 percent

02:50.186 --> 02:52.256 align:middle
at the end of this
year and declining

02:52.256 --> 02:55.206 align:middle
to 3.5 percent by
the end of 2023.

02:56.736 --> 03:00.176 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

03:00.696 --> 03:03.056 align:middle
and those least able to
shoulder the burden have been

03:03.056 --> 03:03.716 align:middle
hardest hit.

03:04.676 --> 03:08.066 align:middle
In particular, despite
progress, joblessness continues

03:08.066 --> 03:10.676 align:middle
to fall disproportionately
on lower-wage workers

03:10.676 --> 03:16.026 align:middle
in the service sector and on
African Americans and Hispanics.

03:16.026 --> 03:18.326 align:middle
Inflation has increased
notably in recent months.

03:18.926 --> 03:23.006 align:middle
The 12-month change in PCE
prices was 3.6 percent in April

03:23.336 --> 03:24.956 align:middle
and will likely remain elevated

03:24.956 --> 03:26.576 align:middle
in coming months
before moderating.

03:27.716 --> 03:30.646 align:middle
Part of the increase reflects
the very low readings from early

03:30.646 --> 03:33.806 align:middle
in the pandemic falling out
of the calculation as well

03:33.806 --> 03:36.516 align:middle
as the pass-through of past
increases in oil prices to,

03:36.616 --> 03:38.186 align:middle
to consumer energy prices.

03:39.506 --> 03:41.996 align:middle
Beyond these effects, we are
also seeing upward pressure

03:41.996 --> 03:43.886 align:middle
on prices from the
rebound in spending

03:44.406 --> 03:47.686 align:middle
as the economy continues
to reopen, particularly

03:47.686 --> 03:50.656 align:middle
as supply bottlenecks have
limited how quickly production

03:50.656 --> 03:52.996 align:middle
in some sectors can
respond in the near term.

03:54.206 --> 03:56.966 align:middle
These bottleneck effects have
been larger than anticipated,

03:57.566 --> 03:59.836 align:middle
and FOMC participants
have revised

03:59.836 --> 04:03.126 align:middle
up their projections for, for
inflation notably for this year.

04:04.166 --> 04:06.466 align:middle
As these transitory
supply effects abate,

04:06.956 --> 04:10.156 align:middle
inflation is expected to drop
back toward our longer-run goal,

04:10.906 --> 04:12.966 align:middle
and the median inflation
projection falls

04:12.966 --> 04:17.186 align:middle
from 3.4 percent this year
to 2.1 percent next year

04:17.456 --> 04:20.126 align:middle
and 2.2 percent in 2023.

04:20.676 --> 04:24.726 align:middle
The process of reopening the
economy is unprecedented,

04:24.906 --> 04:27.276 align:middle
as was the shutdown at
the onset of the pandemic.

04:28.126 --> 04:31.636 align:middle
As the reopening continues,
shifts in demand can be large

04:31.636 --> 04:34.436 align:middle
and rapid, and bottlenecks,
hiring difficulties,

04:34.436 --> 04:36.376 align:middle
and other constraints
could continue

04:36.376 --> 04:40.376 align:middle
to limit how quickly supply can
adjust, raising the possibility

04:40.376 --> 04:42.296 align:middle
that inflation could
turn out to be higher

04:42.486 --> 04:44.076 align:middle
and more persistent
than we expect.

04:45.386 --> 04:48.686 align:middle
Our new framework for monetary
policy emphasizes the importance

04:48.836 --> 04:51.206 align:middle
of having well-anchored
inflation expectations,

04:51.806 --> 04:55.006 align:middle
both to foster price stability
and to enhance our ability

04:55.006 --> 04:56.496 align:middle
to promote our broad-based

04:56.496 --> 04:58.626 align:middle
and inclusive maximum-employment
goal.

05:00.206 --> 05:02.916 align:middle
Indicators of longer-term
inflation expectations have

05:02.916 --> 05:06.166 align:middle
generally reversed the declines
seen earlier in the pandemic

05:06.516 --> 05:09.306 align:middle
and have moved into a range
that appears broadly consistent

05:09.546 --> 05:12.006 align:middle
with our longer-run
inflation goal of 2 percent.

05:13.326 --> 05:15.426 align:middle
If we saw signs that
the path of inflation

05:15.426 --> 05:17.976 align:middle
or longer-term inflation
expectations were moving

05:17.976 --> 05:20.766 align:middle
materially and persistently
beyond levels consistent

05:20.766 --> 05:22.816 align:middle
with our goal, we'd be prepared

05:22.816 --> 05:24.636 align:middle
to adjust the stance
of monetary policy.

05:25.956 --> 05:29.886 align:middle
The pandemic continues to pose
risks to the economic outlook.

05:30.596 --> 05:34.246 align:middle
Progress on vaccinations has
limited the spread of COVID-19

05:34.716 --> 05:36.796 align:middle
and will likely continue
to reduce the effects

05:36.796 --> 05:38.876 align:middle
of the public health
crisis on the economy.

05:39.746 --> 05:41.916 align:middle
However, the pace of
vaccinations has slowed,

05:42.476 --> 05:44.536 align:middle
and new strains of the
virus remain a risk.

05:45.346 --> 05:46.186 align:middle
Continued progress

05:46.186 --> 05:48.036 align:middle
on vaccinations will
support a return

05:48.036 --> 05:49.946 align:middle
to more normal economic
conditions.

05:50.436 --> 05:54.536 align:middle
The Fed's policy actions have
been guided by our mandate

05:54.536 --> 05:57.496 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

05:57.496 --> 06:00.266 align:middle
for the American people, along
with our responsibilities

06:00.266 --> 06:02.426 align:middle
to promote the stability
of the financial system.

06:03.646 --> 06:06.136 align:middle
As the Committee reiterated
in today's policy statement,

06:06.626 --> 06:09.206 align:middle
with inflation having run
persistently below 2 percent,

06:09.496 --> 06:12.346 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

06:12.346 --> 06:15.496 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

06:15.906 --> 06:18.576 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

06:18.576 --> 06:19.556 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

06:20.666 --> 06:22.856 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

06:22.856 --> 06:24.876 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

06:24.876 --> 06:26.586 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

06:27.536 --> 06:30.096 align:middle
With regard to interest
rates, we continue to expect

06:30.096 --> 06:33.186 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
to maintain the current 0

06:33.186 --> 06:35.756 align:middle
to ¼ percent target range
for the federal funds rate

06:36.086 --> 06:38.976 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

06:38.976 --> 06:41.026 align:middle
with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

06:41.546 --> 06:44.516 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

06:44.516 --> 06:46.896 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

06:47.926 --> 06:51.086 align:middle
As is evident in the SEP,
many participants forecast

06:51.086 --> 06:53.766 align:middle
that these favorable economic
conditions will be met somewhat

06:53.766 --> 06:56.986 align:middle
sooner than previously
projected; the median projection

06:56.986 --> 06:59.696 align:middle
for the appropriate level of
the federal funds rate now lies

06:59.696 --> 07:02.576 align:middle
above the effective
lower bound in 2023.

07:03.636 --> 07:05.986 align:middle
Of course, these projections
do not represent a Committee

07:05.986 --> 07:08.726 align:middle
decision or plan, and no
one knows with any certainty

07:08.726 --> 07:11.306 align:middle
where the economy will be
a couple of years from now.

07:12.506 --> 07:14.706 align:middle
More important than any
forecast is the fact that,

07:14.706 --> 07:16.416 align:middle
whenever liftoff comes,

07:16.656 --> 07:18.516 align:middle
policy will remain
highly accommodative.

07:19.746 --> 07:22.056 align:middle
Reaching the conditions for
liftoff will mainly signal

07:22.056 --> 07:23.366 align:middle
that the recovery is strong

07:23.366 --> 07:25.976 align:middle
and no longer requires
holding rates near zero.

07:26.516 --> 07:30.836 align:middle
In addition, we are continuing
to increase-continuing

07:30.836 --> 07:33.066 align:middle
to increase our holdings
of Treasury securities

07:33.116 --> 07:34.916 align:middle
by at least $80 billion
per month

07:35.496 --> 07:37.436 align:middle
and of agency mortgage-backed
securities

07:37.436 --> 07:39.606 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

07:40.106 --> 07:42.786 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

07:42.786 --> 07:45.186 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

07:46.016 --> 07:47.226 align:middle
The increase in our
balance sheet

07:47.226 --> 07:50.446 align:middle
since March 2020 has materially
eased financial conditions

07:50.836 --> 07:53.386 align:middle
and is providing substantial
support to the economy.

07:54.686 --> 07:56.596 align:middle
At our meeting that
concluded earlier today,

07:56.706 --> 07:57.826 align:middle
the Committee had a discussion

07:57.996 --> 08:00.276 align:middle
on the progress made
toward our goals

08:00.586 --> 08:03.316 align:middle
since the Committee adopted
its asset purchase guidance

08:03.316 --> 08:04.056 align:middle
last December.

08:05.106 --> 08:06.226 align:middle
While reaching the standard

08:06.226 --> 08:09.056 align:middle
of "substantial further
progress" is still a ways off,

08:09.636 --> 08:11.716 align:middle
participants expect that
progress will continue.

08:12.666 --> 08:14.566 align:middle
In coming meetings, the
Committee will continue

08:14.566 --> 08:17.076 align:middle
to assess the economy's
progress toward our goals.

08:17.896 --> 08:21.336 align:middle
As we have said, we will provide
advance notice before announcing

08:21.336 --> 08:23.576 align:middle
any decision to make
changes to our purchases.

08:24.086 --> 08:28.196 align:middle
On a final note, we made a
technical adjustment today

08:28.276 --> 08:30.036 align:middle
to the Federal Reserve's
administered rates.

08:30.506 --> 08:33.686 align:middle
The IOER and overnight RRP
rates were adjusted upward

08:33.686 --> 08:37.336 align:middle
by 5 basis points in order to
keep the federal funds rate well

08:37.336 --> 08:38.516 align:middle
within the target range

08:38.596 --> 08:41.296 align:middle
and to support smooth
functioning in money markets.

08:42.346 --> 08:45.266 align:middle
This technical adjustment has no
bearing on the appropriate path

08:45.266 --> 08:48.716 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
or stance of monetary policy.

08:49.296 --> 08:53.616 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

08:53.676 --> 08:55.816 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

08:56.786 --> 08:59.306 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:00.316 --> 09:03.166 align:middle
We at the Fed will do everything
we can to support the economy

09:03.246 --> 09:05.276 align:middle
for as long as it takes
to complete the recovery.

09:06.226 --> 09:06.716 align:middle
Thank you.

09:06.926 --> 09:08.136 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

09:08.826 --> 09:11.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:13.876 --> 09:16.056 align:middle
Rachel Siegel, the
Washington Post.

09:16.056 --> 09:16.756 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

09:16.756 --> 09:19.376 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle, and
thank you, Chair Powell,

09:19.376 --> 09:20.756 align:middle
for taking our questions.

09:20.756 --> 09:23.806 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can walk us
through expectations you have,

09:23.806 --> 09:24.986 align:middle
specifically when it comes

09:24.986 --> 09:27.266 align:middle
to the labor market
going into 2023.

09:27.556 --> 09:30.216 align:middle
And I'm curious about people who
may have left the labor market,

09:30.216 --> 09:31.786 align:middle
who have yet to come back,

09:31.826 --> 09:34.916 align:middle
or who may face issues
with childcare.

09:34.916 --> 09:36.536 align:middle
Perhaps they've retired early.

09:36.986 --> 09:38.936 align:middle
Any barriers that you
see in keeping people

09:38.936 --> 09:40.076 align:middle
from the labor market

09:40.076 --> 09:44.356 align:middle
as you consider full
employment going into 2023?

09:44.356 --> 09:44.866 align:middle
Thank you.

09:44.866 --> 09:45.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:45.206 --> 09:45.806 align:middle
Thank you.

09:46.186 --> 09:51.016 align:middle
So I would say, if you look at
the labor market and you look

09:51.016 --> 09:55.916 align:middle
at the demand for workers
and the level of job creation

09:55.916 --> 09:59.266 align:middle
and think ahead, I think it's
clear, and I am confident,

09:59.266 --> 10:02.486 align:middle
that we are on a path to a very
strong labor market-a labor

10:02.486 --> 10:04.946 align:middle
market that, that
shows low unemployment,

10:04.986 --> 10:09.106 align:middle
high participation, rising wages
for people across the spectrum.

10:09.106 --> 10:11.536 align:middle
I mean, I think that's, that's
shown in our projections,

10:11.536 --> 10:13.506 align:middle
it's shown in outside
projections.

10:13.506 --> 10:16.166 align:middle
And if you look through the
current time frame and think one

10:16.166 --> 10:19.006 align:middle
and two years out, we're
going to be looking at a very,

10:19.006 --> 10:21.926 align:middle
very strong labor market.

10:23.296 --> 10:25.236 align:middle
In terms of exactly
what that means, we'll,

10:25.666 --> 10:27.346 align:middle
we'll have to see
how things evolve.

10:27.346 --> 10:29.066 align:middle
I think we learned
during the course

10:29.066 --> 10:32.316 align:middle
of the last very long expansion,
the longest in our history,

10:32.796 --> 10:37.156 align:middle
that labor supply during a
long expansion can exceed

10:37.156 --> 10:39.866 align:middle
expectations, can move
above its estimated trend.

10:39.866 --> 10:41.046 align:middle
And, and I have no
reason to think

10:41.046 --> 10:42.906 align:middle
that that won't happen again.

10:43.516 --> 10:46.996 align:middle
At the same time, we have
seen-in terms of participation,

10:46.996 --> 10:50.336 align:middle
we've seen a significant
number of, of people retire.

10:51.146 --> 10:56.046 align:middle
And so we, we don't actually
know exactly what labor force

10:56.166 --> 11:00.756 align:middle
participation will be as we go
forward, but I, I would tend to,

11:00.846 --> 11:02.966 align:middle
to look at it and think
that it-that it can return

11:02.966 --> 11:05.596 align:middle
to high levels, although it
may take some time to do that.

11:05.976 --> 11:08.556 align:middle
But overall, this is-this
is going to be, you know,

11:08.726 --> 11:10.076 align:middle
a very strong labor market.

11:10.076 --> 11:13.416 align:middle
In terms of the near term, you
ask as well-so we see a couple

11:13.416 --> 11:15.876 align:middle
of things, a few
things that seem likely

11:15.876 --> 11:17.836 align:middle
to be holding back labor supply.

11:17.836 --> 11:19.726 align:middle
There are very large
amounts of job openings,

11:19.726 --> 11:21.516 align:middle
and there are a very
large number

11:21.516 --> 11:23.246 align:middle
of people who, who
are unemployed.

11:23.686 --> 11:28.236 align:middle
And the pace of, of filling
those jobs is-somehow feels

11:28.236 --> 11:29.326 align:middle
slower than it might be.

11:29.326 --> 11:30.566 align:middle
So I'd point to a
number of things,

11:31.006 --> 11:35.016 align:middle
the first of which is just that
most of the, the act of sort

11:35.016 --> 11:37.326 align:middle
of going back to one's
old job-that's kind

11:37.326 --> 11:38.056 align:middle
of already happened.

11:38.056 --> 11:40.896 align:middle
So this is a question of
people finding a new job.

11:41.456 --> 11:43.606 align:middle
And that's just a
process that takes longer.

11:43.606 --> 11:46.866 align:middle
There may be something
of a speed limit on it.

11:46.866 --> 11:50.136 align:middle
You've got to find a job where,
where your skills match what,

11:50.136 --> 11:52.166 align:middle
you know, what, what
the employer wants.

11:52.166 --> 11:53.426 align:middle
It's got to be in
the right area.

11:54.056 --> 11:55.666 align:middle
There's just a lot that goes

11:55.666 --> 11:57.346 align:middle
into the function
of finding a job.

11:57.346 --> 11:59.386 align:middle
So that's, that's sort
of a natural thing.

11:59.746 --> 12:05.476 align:middle
In addition, I would say that
we, we look at, for example, a,

12:05.516 --> 12:09.456 align:middle
a significant number of people
still say that they're concerned

12:09.456 --> 12:12.156 align:middle
about going back to work
in jobs where there's a lot

12:12.156 --> 12:14.926 align:middle
of public facing because
of-because of COVID.

12:15.246 --> 12:17.136 align:middle
So that's clearly
holding back some people,

12:17.136 --> 12:22.386 align:middle
and that should diminish
as vaccinations move ahead.

12:23.286 --> 12:25.646 align:middle
There's also the
question of childcare.

12:25.646 --> 12:27.926 align:middle
Many are engaged
in, in caretaking.

12:28.426 --> 12:31.176 align:middle
And as schools reopen and, and,

12:31.476 --> 12:35.856 align:middle
and childcare/daycare centers
open in the fall-in the fall,

12:35.856 --> 12:37.666 align:middle
then we should see-we should see

12:37.666 --> 12:40.576 align:middle
that supporting labor force
participation by caretakers.

12:40.866 --> 12:44.596 align:middle
Finally, unemployment
insurance for something

12:44.596 --> 12:47.826 align:middle
like 15 million people will
either end or be diminished

12:48.346 --> 12:52.566 align:middle
as we move through the summer
and into, into, into the fall

12:52.566 --> 12:54.786 align:middle
by the end of September, and I'd

12:55.536 --> 12:59.566 align:middle
like some-that may
also encourage some

12:59.566 --> 13:01.006 align:middle
to go back in and take jobs.

13:01.006 --> 13:03.736 align:middle
So you would think that that
would add to an increase

13:03.736 --> 13:04.806 align:middle
in job creation as well.

13:04.806 --> 13:07.086 align:middle
So you put all those
together, I would expect

13:07.086 --> 13:10.196 align:middle
that we would see strong
job creation building

13:10.196 --> 13:13.636 align:middle
up over the summer and going
into-going into the fall.

13:13.766 --> 13:16.396 align:middle
I will also say, though,
the last thing I'll say is,

13:17.276 --> 13:20.536 align:middle
this is an extraordinarily
unusual time.

13:20.756 --> 13:25.116 align:middle
And we, we really don't have
a template or, or, you know,

13:25.116 --> 13:29.036 align:middle
any experience of, of
a situation like this.

13:29.036 --> 13:31.076 align:middle
And so I think we have to
be humble about our ability

13:31.076 --> 13:32.126 align:middle
to understand the data.

13:32.446 --> 13:35.226 align:middle
It's not a time to try
to reach hard conclusions

13:35.676 --> 13:37.706 align:middle
about the labor market,
about inflation,

13:37.706 --> 13:38.896 align:middle
about the path of policy.

13:39.306 --> 13:40.846 align:middle
We need to see more data.

13:40.846 --> 13:42.196 align:middle
We need to be a little
bit patient.

13:42.526 --> 13:44.246 align:middle
And I do think, though,
that we'll, we'll,

13:44.346 --> 13:47.736 align:middle
we'll be seeing some things
coming up in coming months

13:47.736 --> 13:49.806 align:middle
that will-that will
inform our, our thinking.

13:50.206 --> 13:50.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:54.016 --> 13:54.496 align:middle
Thank you.

13:54.586 --> 13:55.116 align:middle
Paul Kiernan.

13:55.116 --> 13:58.416 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

13:58.416 --> 13:59.696 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

13:59.696 --> 14:01.086 align:middle
Thanks for the question.

14:01.086 --> 14:05.486 align:middle
Your-the Committee's median
forecast on inflation seems

14:05.486 --> 14:07.756 align:middle
to assume a pretty,
pretty tame outlook

14:07.756 --> 14:08.886 align:middle
for the rest of the year.

14:09.446 --> 14:12.896 align:middle
As you know, the
three-month annualized rate

14:12.896 --> 14:17.116 align:middle
for the past three months was,
I think, 8.4 percent in the CPI.

14:17.116 --> 14:19.586 align:middle
And I'm just wondering sort

14:19.586 --> 14:22.036 align:middle
of how much longer
we can sustain those,

14:22.036 --> 14:23.996 align:middle
those kinds of rates
before you get nervous.

14:24.096 --> 14:26.486 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

14:28.086 --> 14:30.916 align:middle
So inflation has come
in above expectations

14:30.916 --> 14:31.966 align:middle
over the last few months.

14:32.606 --> 14:34.396 align:middle
But if you look behind
the headline numbers,

14:34.396 --> 14:37.026 align:middle
you'll see that the incoming
data are, are consistent

14:37.026 --> 14:39.296 align:middle
with the view that prices-that
prices that are driving

14:39.296 --> 14:41.466 align:middle
that higher inflation
are from categories

14:41.466 --> 14:43.626 align:middle
that are being directly
affected by the recovery

14:44.046 --> 14:46.096 align:middle
from the pandemic and the
reopening of the economy.

14:46.856 --> 14:52.766 align:middle
So, for example, the experience
with, with lumber prices is,

14:52.766 --> 14:54.136 align:middle
is illustrative of this.

14:54.166 --> 14:58.466 align:middle
The thought is that prices
like that that have moved

14:58.466 --> 15:01.796 align:middle
up really quickly because of
the shortages and bottlenecks

15:01.796 --> 15:04.616 align:middle
and the like, they should stop
going up, and at some point,

15:04.616 --> 15:06.696 align:middle
they, they, in some cases,
should actually go down.

15:06.696 --> 15:08.266 align:middle
And we did see that
in the case of lumber.

15:08.746 --> 15:11.496 align:middle
Another example where we
haven't seen that yet is prices

15:11.496 --> 15:14.266 align:middle
for used cars, which
accounted for more than a third

15:14.266 --> 15:15.976 align:middle
of the total increase
in core inflation.

15:16.486 --> 15:18.536 align:middle
Used car prices are
going up because of sort

15:18.536 --> 15:20.606 align:middle
of a perfect storm
of very strong demand

15:20.606 --> 15:21.666 align:middle
and limited supply.

15:22.146 --> 15:24.626 align:middle
It's going up at just
an amazing annual rate.

15:25.126 --> 15:28.666 align:middle
But we do think that it makes
sense that that would stop,

15:28.666 --> 15:30.436 align:middle
and that in fact it
would reverse over time.

15:30.436 --> 15:31.926 align:middle
So we think we'll be
seeing some of that.

15:32.006 --> 15:33.446 align:middle
When will we be seeing it?

15:33.446 --> 15:34.726 align:middle
We're not sure.

15:34.976 --> 15:37.566 align:middle
That narrative seems,
still seems quite likely

15:37.566 --> 15:39.686 align:middle
to prove correct, although,
you know, as I pointed

15:39.686 --> 15:42.166 align:middle
out at the last press
conference, the,

15:42.166 --> 15:47.006 align:middle
the timing of that is, is pretty
uncertain, and so are the,

15:47.006 --> 15:48.146 align:middle
the effects in the near term.

15:48.146 --> 15:52.426 align:middle
But over time, it seems likely
that these very specific things

15:52.426 --> 15:56.336 align:middle
that are driving up inflation
will be-will be temporary.

15:56.736 --> 16:00.336 align:middle
And we'll be, you know,
we're going to be looking.

16:00.336 --> 16:01.886 align:middle
We'll be looking at the
monthly pricing data.

16:01.976 --> 16:05.226 align:middle
I'll, I'll also say that
the labor market is going

16:05.226 --> 16:08.076 align:middle
to be important, both for
the maximum-employment goal,

16:09.166 --> 16:11.196 align:middle
but also for inflation.

16:11.396 --> 16:12.596 align:middle
And we'll be looking at that.

16:12.816 --> 16:16.406 align:middle
And, and as I-as I mentioned,
we expect and I expect

16:16.406 --> 16:20.286 align:middle
that we'll see increases in
supply over coming months

16:20.366 --> 16:23.176 align:middle
as the factors that we believe
have been suppressing supply

16:24.216 --> 16:26.696 align:middle
abate, wane, move down.

16:27.396 --> 16:30.906 align:middle
So I, I can't give you an
exact number or an exact time,

16:31.096 --> 16:34.866 align:middle
but I would say that we do
expect inflation to move down.

16:34.866 --> 16:38.856 align:middle
If you look at the-if
you look at the forecast

16:38.856 --> 16:44.996 align:middle
for 2021 and-sorry, 2022 and
2023 among my colleagues on the,

16:44.996 --> 16:46.746 align:middle
on the Federal Open Market
Committee, you will see

16:46.746 --> 16:48.916 align:middle
that people do expect
inflation to move

16:48.916 --> 16:50.336 align:middle
down meaningfully
toward our goal.

16:50.756 --> 16:55.486 align:middle
And I think the full range of,
of, of inflation projections

16:55.486 --> 17:00.376 align:middle
for 2023 falls between 2 and
2.3 percent, which is consistent

17:00.376 --> 17:02.356 align:middle
with our-with our goals.

17:02.596 --> 17:05.726 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:05.726 --> 17:07.686 align:middle
Thank you.

17:07.686 --> 17:09.766 align:middle
Now we'll go to Ylan
Mui at CNBC.

17:09.766 --> 17:11.856 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Hi.

17:12.096 --> 17:14.796 align:middle
Thank you, Chair
Powell, for doing this.

17:14.886 --> 17:16.586 align:middle
My question for you
is that you mentioned

17:16.586 --> 17:20.166 align:middle
that your colleagues did have
a discussion about the progress

17:20.166 --> 17:25.476 align:middle
that you're making toward
your, your goals in order

17:25.476 --> 17:27.596 align:middle
to consider tapering
your asset purchases.

17:27.596 --> 17:29.716 align:middle
In that discussion, you said

17:29.716 --> 17:31.926 align:middle
that you didn't-haven't made
substantial progress yet,

17:31.926 --> 17:34.506 align:middle
but that you expect to
continue to make progress.

17:34.506 --> 17:37.716 align:middle
In that discussion, did you
guys talk about a timeline

17:37.716 --> 17:40.356 align:middle
for when you expect to
see that progress be made

17:40.506 --> 17:43.776 align:middle
and when you might
consider starting

17:43.776 --> 17:44.776 align:middle
to reduce those purchases?

17:44.776 --> 17:46.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:46.046 --> 17:50.326 align:middle
Right. So I, I expect that
we'll be able to say more

17:50.326 --> 17:52.396 align:middle
about timing as we
see more data.

17:52.626 --> 17:53.846 align:middle
Basically, there's not a lot

17:54.016 --> 17:55.966 align:middle
of more light I can-I
can shed on that.

17:56.546 --> 17:58.456 align:middle
But you can think of this
meeting that we had as the

17:58.456 --> 18:00.656 align:middle
"talking about talking
about" meeting, if you like.

18:01.266 --> 18:03.696 align:middle
And I now suggest that
we retire that term,

18:03.696 --> 18:05.746 align:middle
which has-which has served
its purpose well, I think.

18:06.436 --> 18:08.886 align:middle
So Committee participants
were of the view

18:08.886 --> 18:12.426 align:middle
that since we adopted
that guidance in December,

18:12.426 --> 18:14.326 align:middle
the economy has clearly
made progress,

18:14.326 --> 18:16.416 align:middle
although we are still
a ways from our goal

18:16.416 --> 18:18.236 align:middle
of substantial further progress.

18:18.856 --> 18:22.426 align:middle
Participants expect continued
progress ahead toward

18:22.426 --> 18:22.986 align:middle
that objective.

18:22.986 --> 18:25.486 align:middle
And assuming that is the
case, it will be appropriate

18:25.486 --> 18:26.956 align:middle
to consider announcing a plan

18:26.956 --> 18:29.866 align:middle
for reducing our asset
purchases at a future meeting.

18:29.986 --> 18:32.476 align:middle
So at coming meetings, the
Committee will continue

18:32.476 --> 18:34.736 align:middle
to assess the economy's
progress toward our goals,

18:35.016 --> 18:38.776 align:middle
and we'll give advance notice
before announcing any decision.

18:39.036 --> 18:42.516 align:middle
The timing, of course, Ylan,
will depend on the pace

18:42.516 --> 18:44.266 align:middle
of that progress and
not on any calendar.

18:44.716 --> 18:47.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:48.496 --> 18:49.056 align:middle
Thank you.

18:49.056 --> 18:51.156 align:middle
Now we'll go to Chris
Rugaber, AP.

18:53.756 --> 18:55.766 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

18:55.766 --> 18:57.856 align:middle
Right. Thank you.

18:58.056 --> 19:01.736 align:middle
Well, you mentioned-let me ask
about inflation expectations.

19:01.736 --> 19:04.916 align:middle
You said they were-I
think you mentioned

19:04.916 --> 19:08.116 align:middle
in your opening statement that
you saw them as within target.

19:08.566 --> 19:12.626 align:middle
Does that mean that some of the
shorter-term measures we've seen

19:12.626 --> 19:15.056 align:middle
out there such as the New York
Federal Reserve's three-year

19:15.056 --> 19:18.536 align:middle
outlook, which jumped
a bit-should those sort

19:18.536 --> 19:19.516 align:middle
of be dismissed?

19:19.996 --> 19:21.446 align:middle
And are we only looking

19:21.446 --> 19:24.026 align:middle
at longer-term inflation
expectations?

19:24.026 --> 19:25.676 align:middle
And would you describe those

19:25.676 --> 19:27.966 align:middle
as still well anchored
at this point?

19:27.966 --> 19:31.616 align:middle
And on a related note, would
the Fed consider publishing its

19:31.616 --> 19:34.806 align:middle
index of common inflation
expectations on a monthly basis?

19:35.006 --> 19:35.376 align:middle
Thank you.

19:35.426 --> 19:37.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:38.386 --> 19:39.666 align:middle
So we, we do tend to look

19:39.666 --> 19:42.566 align:middle
at the longer-term
inflation expectations,

19:42.626 --> 19:47.816 align:middle
because that's really, we think,
what matters for, for inflation.

19:48.966 --> 19:53.216 align:middle
So and, and, you know, the
shorter-term ones do tend

19:53.216 --> 19:56.396 align:middle
to move around based on, for
example, gasoline prices.

19:56.396 --> 19:59.186 align:middle
So you'll see if gasoline
prices were to spike,

19:59.186 --> 20:03.266 align:middle
you'll see the shorter-term
inflation expectation measures,

20:03.266 --> 20:05.326 align:middle
particularly the
surveys, move up.

20:05.916 --> 20:09.426 align:middle
And, and that's, that's
maybe not a good signal

20:09.426 --> 20:12.616 align:middle
for future inflation if,
if gas happens to spike

20:12.616 --> 20:15.706 align:middle
and then go back down again.

20:15.806 --> 20:20.316 align:middle
So we-yes, I think if, if
you look at the broad range

20:20.396 --> 20:24.596 align:middle
of longer-term inflation
expectations, they've moved up.

20:24.956 --> 20:28.056 align:middle
They moved down during the
beginning of the pandemic,

20:28.286 --> 20:30.636 align:middle
you know, sort of further
exacerbating concerns

20:30.676 --> 20:34.786 align:middle
that we might find ourselves
where, for example, the ECB

20:34.786 --> 20:37.866 align:middle
and the Bank of Japan have been,
where you have expectations

20:37.866 --> 20:39.986 align:middle
and inflation itself
sliding down

20:39.986 --> 20:42.366 align:middle
and you have a really
hard time stopping

20:42.366 --> 20:44.056 align:middle
that process once it begins.

20:44.716 --> 20:45.776 align:middle
So that was a concern.

20:46.126 --> 20:47.596 align:middle
So it's, it's good, actually,

20:47.596 --> 20:50.796 align:middle
to see inflation-longer-term
inflation expectations move back

20:50.796 --> 20:53.716 align:middle
up to a range-it's a
range that's consistent

20:53.716 --> 20:56.136 align:middle
with what our objectives are.

20:56.216 --> 20:58.986 align:middle
These are not precise
measures, and that's-and they,

20:58.986 --> 21:01.836 align:middle
they contain risk
premiums of various kinds.

21:01.836 --> 21:03.936 align:middle
And that's why we look at a
broad range of them and tend

21:03.936 --> 21:08.116 align:middle
to look at the movement of
that broad range of, of,

21:08.266 --> 21:11.686 align:middle
of indicators, which are from,
you know, surveys of economists,

21:11.686 --> 21:13.796 align:middle
surveys of the public,
and also market based.

21:14.036 --> 21:17.246 align:middle
It's, it's, it's a wide
index, as I'm sure you know.

21:17.986 --> 21:19.486 align:middle
We look at that,
and we see them back

21:19.486 --> 21:21.066 align:middle
in the range where they were.

21:21.066 --> 21:25.216 align:middle
And, by the way, they've been
broadly higher than that,

21:25.216 --> 21:27.936 align:middle
somewhat modestly higher than
that, not so many years ago,

21:27.936 --> 21:31.606 align:middle
at a time when inflation
was, was, was still anchored

21:31.806 --> 21:34.826 align:middle
at around 2 percent or maybe
even a little bit below.

21:34.826 --> 21:38.186 align:middle
So the answer is, yes, I think
they are anchored and they're

21:38.186 --> 21:39.316 align:middle
at a good place right now.

21:39.316 --> 21:41.416 align:middle
It's gratifying to see
them having moved up off

21:41.416 --> 21:43.316 align:middle
of their pandemic lows.

21:43.916 --> 21:46.436 align:middle
And, you know, as you know,
it's, it's fundamental

21:46.436 --> 21:49.856 align:middle
in our framework, our
new framework, to,

21:49.856 --> 21:51.626 align:middle
to assure

21:51.626 --> 21:55.226 align:middle
that inflation-longer-term
inflation expectations are

21:55.226 --> 21:58.256 align:middle
anchored at a place that is
consistent with our goal.

21:58.256 --> 22:01.426 align:middle
We, we think that's
an important reason.

22:01.426 --> 22:03.706 align:middle
If, if inflation
expectations are not anchored

22:03.706 --> 22:05.466 align:middle
at a place that's
consistent with your goal,

22:06.076 --> 22:07.276 align:middle
it's not clear why
you would expect

22:07.276 --> 22:08.816 align:middle
to hit your goal
over the longer term.

22:09.316 --> 22:10.156 align:middle
So it's important.

22:10.266 --> 22:10.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMTH.

22:12.706 --> 22:15.226 align:middle
Okay, now we'll go to
James Politi with the FT.

22:15.226 --> 22:15.646 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

22:18.296 --> 22:21.256 align:middle
Chair Powell-thanks,
Chair Powell.

22:21.256 --> 22:25.406 align:middle
Your economic projections
today forecast 7 percent growth

22:25.406 --> 22:29.186 align:middle
in 2022, unemployment
at 4½ percent,

22:29.186 --> 22:31.256 align:middle
and core inflation of 3 percent.

22:31.346 --> 22:34.056 align:middle
If those conditions are
achieved by the end of the year,

22:34.396 --> 22:37.636 align:middle
would that constitute
substantial further progress,

22:38.166 --> 22:39.096 align:middle
in your mind?

22:39.256 --> 22:42.996 align:middle
And kind of more broadly,
when you look at the sort

22:42.996 --> 22:47.126 align:middle
of median forecast for interest
rates in 2023 showing not one

22:47.126 --> 22:51.566 align:middle
but two interest rate
increases at the time, I mean,

22:51.566 --> 22:54.166 align:middle
is this kind of-can you
describe the sort of tone

22:54.166 --> 22:57.066 align:middle
of the-of the discussion
in the Committee?

22:57.066 --> 23:01.566 align:middle
And are we really moving towards
sort of a post-pandemic stance?

23:01.566 --> 23:04.036 align:middle
Is there greater
confidence that, you know,

23:04.336 --> 23:07.016 align:middle
the recovery will be, you know,

23:07.066 --> 23:09.116 align:middle
a full recovery sooner
than expected?

23:09.116 --> 23:10.086 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:10.086 --> 23:13.556 align:middle
On your first question, the
judgment of when we have arrived

23:13.556 --> 23:15.386 align:middle
at substantial further
progress is one

23:15.386 --> 23:16.396 align:middle
that the Committee will make.

23:16.396 --> 23:19.346 align:middle
And it would not be
appropriate for me to lay

23:19.346 --> 23:20.826 align:middle
out particular numbers that do

23:20.826 --> 23:23.356 align:middle
or do not-that do
or do not qualify.

23:23.356 --> 23:24.596 align:middle
That is-that is, you know,

23:24.596 --> 23:28.306 align:middle
the process that we're beginning
now at the next meeting.

23:28.306 --> 23:30.486 align:middle
We will begin, meeting
by meeting, to,

23:31.036 --> 23:32.696 align:middle
to assess that progress and talk

23:32.696 --> 23:35.646 align:middle
about what we-what we
think we're seeing and,

23:35.646 --> 23:38.256 align:middle
and just do all of the
things that you do to sort

23:38.256 --> 23:43.446 align:middle
of clarify your thinking around
the process of deciding whether

23:43.446 --> 23:46.836 align:middle
and how to adjust the pace and
composition of asset purchases.

23:47.626 --> 23:54.136 align:middle
In terms of the, the, the two
hikes-so let me say a couple

23:54.136 --> 23:55.926 align:middle
things first of all,
not for the first time,

23:55.926 --> 23:57.256 align:middle
about the-about the dot plot.

23:57.856 --> 23:59.856 align:middle
These are, of course,
individual projections.

23:59.856 --> 24:02.206 align:middle
They're not a Committee
forecast, they're not a plan.

24:02.876 --> 24:05.206 align:middle
And we did not actually
have a discussion

24:05.446 --> 24:07.546 align:middle
of whether liftoff
is appropriate

24:07.546 --> 24:10.926 align:middle
at any particular year, because
discussing liftoff now would

24:10.926 --> 24:13.246 align:middle
be-would be highly premature,
wouldn't make any sense.

24:13.966 --> 24:17.226 align:middle
If you look at the
transcripts from five years ago,

24:17.226 --> 24:19.766 align:middle
you'll see that sometimes
people mention their rate path

24:19.766 --> 24:20.666 align:middle
in their interventions.

24:20.856 --> 24:22.786 align:middle
Often they don't.

24:22.876 --> 24:24.456 align:middle
And the last thing to say is,

24:24.806 --> 24:27.546 align:middle
the dots are not a
great forecaster of,

24:27.546 --> 24:28.566 align:middle
of future rate moves.

24:28.566 --> 24:30.236 align:middle
And that's not because-it's just

24:30.236 --> 24:31.586 align:middle
because it's so highly
uncertain.

24:31.586 --> 24:35.476 align:middle
There is no great
forecaster of, of future dots.

24:35.476 --> 24:39.406 align:middle
So, so dots to be taken with
a-with a big, big grain of salt.

24:39.916 --> 24:43.726 align:middle
However, so let me talk about
this, this, this meeting.

24:44.346 --> 24:46.716 align:middle
The Committee spelled
out, as you know,

24:46.716 --> 24:49.286 align:middle
in our FOMC statements the
conditions that it expects

24:49.286 --> 24:52.636 align:middle
to see before an adjustment
in the target range is made.

24:53.396 --> 24:55.266 align:middle
And it's outcome based,
it's not time based.

24:55.266 --> 24:58.146 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, it's labor
market conditions consistent

24:58.146 --> 25:00.596 align:middle
with maximum employment,
inflation at 2 percent

25:01.076 --> 25:02.896 align:middle
and on track to exceed
2 percent.

25:03.456 --> 25:05.296 align:middle
In the projections,
it gives some sense

25:05.296 --> 25:07.596 align:middle
of how participants see
the economy evolving

25:07.596 --> 25:08.816 align:middle
in their most likely case.

25:09.236 --> 25:11.436 align:middle
And, honestly, the main
message I would take away

25:11.436 --> 25:14.846 align:middle
from the SEP is that
participants-many,

25:14.846 --> 25:17.196 align:middle
many participants
are more comfortable

25:17.506 --> 25:18.756 align:middle
that the economic conditions

25:18.756 --> 25:20.976 align:middle
in the Committee's forward
guidance will be met somewhat

25:20.976 --> 25:22.556 align:middle
sooner than previously
anticipated.

25:23.126 --> 25:24.806 align:middle
And that would be a
welcome development.

25:24.956 --> 25:26.406 align:middle
If such outcomes materialize,

25:26.406 --> 25:29.516 align:middle
it means the economy will have
made faster progress toward

25:29.516 --> 25:29.966 align:middle
our goals.

25:30.846 --> 25:35.876 align:middle
So the other thing I'll say is,
rate increases are really not

25:35.876 --> 25:37.686 align:middle
at all the focus
of the Committee.

25:37.686 --> 25:39.376 align:middle
The focus of the Committee
is the current state

25:39.376 --> 25:40.026 align:middle
of the economy.

25:40.176 --> 25:43.176 align:middle
But in terms of our tools,
it's about asset purchases.

25:43.176 --> 25:44.226 align:middle
That's what we're
thinking about.

25:44.906 --> 25:47.026 align:middle
Liftoff is, is well
into the future.

25:47.026 --> 25:49.686 align:middle
The conditions for
liftoff-we're very far

25:49.686 --> 25:51.246 align:middle
from maximum employment,
for example.

25:51.776 --> 25:53.696 align:middle
It's, it's a consideration
for the future.

25:53.696 --> 25:56.576 align:middle
So the near-term thing is
really-the real near-term

25:56.576 --> 26:00.096 align:middle
discussion, discussion that
will begin is really about the,

26:00.096 --> 26:01.466 align:middle
the path of asset purchases.

26:01.466 --> 26:04.266 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, we had
a discussion about that today

26:04.886 --> 26:07.126 align:middle
and expect to, in
future meetings,

26:07.236 --> 26:09.166 align:middle
continue to think
about our progress.

26:09.216 --> 26:09.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:11.846 --> 26:12.426 align:middle
Thank you.

26:12.426 --> 26:14.266 align:middle
We'll go to Nancy
Marshall-Genzer

26:14.266 --> 26:15.696 align:middle
from Marketplace.

26:16.656 --> 26:17.516 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

26:17.516 --> 26:20.346 align:middle
Continuing-Chair Powell,
continuing in that vein,

26:20.816 --> 26:24.516 align:middle
when you're ready, how will you
go about signaling the start

26:24.516 --> 26:27.036 align:middle
of tapering when you
do decide to do that?

26:29.056 --> 26:29.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:30.406 --> 26:34.116 align:middle
So our intention
for this process is

26:34.116 --> 26:38.696 align:middle
that it will be orderly,
methodical, and transparent.

26:39.706 --> 26:44.986 align:middle
And I can just tell you,
we, we, we see real value

26:45.306 --> 26:48.256 align:middle
in communicating well in
advance what our thinking is.

26:48.956 --> 26:51.746 align:middle
And we'll try to be clear.

26:51.746 --> 26:52.626 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, we'll,

26:52.626 --> 26:58.386 align:middle
we'll give advance notice before
announcing a decision to taper.

26:58.596 --> 27:04.746 align:middle
And so all I can say is that we,
we think it's important-we think

27:04.746 --> 27:07.536 align:middle
where the balance sheet's
concerned, a lot of notice,

27:08.216 --> 27:11.346 align:middle
as much transparency as we
can give, and as far-as far

27:11.346 --> 27:13.666 align:middle
in advance as we can
to give people a chance

27:13.666 --> 27:15.176 align:middle
to adjust their expectations.

27:15.826 --> 27:17.496 align:middle
And, you know, we expect
to be in that business

27:17.496 --> 27:19.456 align:middle
until we reach substantial
further progress

27:19.456 --> 27:20.646 align:middle
and then have a-have a decision.

27:20.746 --> 27:22.426 align:middle
Again, I have nothing
further on time.

27:22.426 --> 27:24.016 align:middle
It wouldn't be appropriate
to say.

27:24.016 --> 27:25.246 align:middle
We're going to have
to see more data.

27:25.946 --> 27:26.816 align:middle
We're a ways away

27:26.816 --> 27:29.456 align:middle
from substantial further
progress, we think.

27:30.096 --> 27:31.266 align:middle
But we're making progress.

27:31.506 --> 27:32.746 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

27:32.746 --> 27:37.166 align:middle
So you can't say generally how
far in advance you would signal?

27:37.246 --> 27:38.306 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:38.386 --> 27:42.356 align:middle
Again, as we-as we approach that
goal, we'll provide, you know,

27:43.336 --> 27:44.986 align:middle
as much clarity as we can.

27:45.456 --> 27:49.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:49.806 --> 27:50.816 align:middle
Great, thank you.

27:51.076 --> 27:52.456 align:middle
Going to Craig Torres
at Bloomberg.

27:52.456 --> 27:55.326 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

27:55.476 --> 27:56.756 align:middle
Craig Torres at Bloomberg.

27:57.216 --> 28:00.696 align:middle
If I were a businessman
looking at the forecast today,

28:00.866 --> 28:04.506 align:middle
I would ask how and when the
Fed seeks to achieve an average

28:04.506 --> 28:05.966 align:middle
of 2 percent inflation.

28:06.146 --> 28:09.616 align:middle
In other words, does the
FOMC have a look-back period?

28:10.306 --> 28:12.746 align:middle
Or does it plan to
suppress inflation

28:12.746 --> 28:15.616 align:middle
in outer years because, over the
next three years, you're going

28:15.616 --> 28:16.916 align:middle
to be above inflation?

28:17.346 --> 28:19.546 align:middle
So what is your look-back
period?

28:19.706 --> 28:21.016 align:middle
Does the Committee have one?

28:21.016 --> 28:22.256 align:middle
And if not, why not?

28:23.046 --> 28:26.546 align:middle
And if they don't, why isn't
this just flexible inflation

28:26.546 --> 28:31.306 align:middle
targeting without an average
in a range of 2 to 2¼ percent?

28:31.436 --> 28:33.646 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

28:33.646 --> 28:37.626 align:middle
You know, so as part of our
year-and-a-half-long process,

28:38.616 --> 28:44.346 align:middle
the review that we did and came
out with at the end of that

28:44.346 --> 28:47.016 align:middle
with the-with the new
Statement on Longer-Run Goals

28:47.016 --> 28:50.456 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we looked carefully at the idea.

28:51.016 --> 28:52.676 align:middle
We've all read all
the literature

28:52.676 --> 28:56.956 align:middle
around different formulas for
makeup and things like that.

28:57.616 --> 28:59.366 align:middle
And we concluded-and, you know,

28:59.366 --> 29:04.916 align:middle
I strongly agree-that it's
not wise to, to wed yourself

29:04.916 --> 29:07.086 align:middle
to a particular formulation
of that.

29:07.266 --> 29:11.636 align:middle
So we did adopt a
discretionary-there's an element

29:11.636 --> 29:13.056 align:middle
of discretion in it.

29:13.406 --> 29:15.746 align:middle
You know, it says that we
will seek to-seek inflation

29:15.746 --> 29:18.366 align:middle
that runs moderately above
2 percent for some time.

29:18.916 --> 29:20.926 align:middle
And it's, it's meant
to create a broad sense

29:20.926 --> 29:23.616 align:middle
that we want inflation to
average 2 percent over time.

29:24.356 --> 29:28.806 align:middle
And that under the
old-under the old formula,

29:28.996 --> 29:32.186 align:middle
under the old framework,
what was happening was,

29:32.226 --> 29:35.796 align:middle
2 percent was a ceiling because
all of the errors were below.

29:35.796 --> 29:37.726 align:middle
You were always getting
back to 2 percent.

29:37.726 --> 29:40.316 align:middle
So you were bouncing back
and forth between 1½ and 2,

29:40.596 --> 29:42.226 align:middle
and we wanted them to
be centered around 2.

29:42.226 --> 29:45.186 align:middle
So, so that's, that's the
approach that we're taking.

29:45.586 --> 29:49.676 align:middle
And you're right, it's not-it's
not a formulaic approach.

29:49.676 --> 29:51.396 align:middle
We were clear on that when
we announced the framework.

29:51.396 --> 29:53.016 align:middle
Was there another part
of your question, Craig?

29:53.456 --> 29:57.086 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

29:57.086 --> 29:58.586 align:middle
That pretty much
answers it, Chair Powell.

29:58.586 --> 29:58.946 align:middle
Thank you.

29:58.946 --> 29:59.596 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:01.996 --> 30:02.626 align:middle
Thank you.

30:02.626 --> 30:03.726 align:middle
Now we'll go to Michael Derby.

30:03.726 --> 30:06.786 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

30:06.786 --> 30:08.336 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

30:08.336 --> 30:11.796 align:middle
So I wanted to ask you
about the reverse repo usage

30:11.796 --> 30:12.576 align:middle
that we've seen lately.

30:12.576 --> 30:16.656 align:middle
I was curious if you are at
all concerned about the level

30:16.656 --> 30:19.456 align:middle
of money flowing into the
reverse repo facility.

30:19.456 --> 30:21.906 align:middle
And do you believe
that the changes

30:21.906 --> 30:25.066 align:middle
in the Fed's rate control
toolkit today will have any

30:25.066 --> 30:26.446 align:middle
impact on that?

30:26.446 --> 30:29.276 align:middle
And then, in a related
question, do you think

30:29.276 --> 30:33.316 align:middle
that Fed asset purchases are
taking too many safe assets

30:33.316 --> 30:34.956 align:middle
out of the market right now

30:34.956 --> 30:36.886 align:middle
and creating maybe
some dislocations

30:36.886 --> 30:38.206 align:middle
in the money markets?

30:38.496 --> 30:39.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:39.536 --> 30:44.246 align:middle
So on the-on the facility,
we think it's doing its job.

30:44.246 --> 30:47.126 align:middle
We think it's doing-the
reverse repo facility is,

30:47.216 --> 30:50.216 align:middle
is doing what it's supposed to
do, which is to provide a floor

30:50.216 --> 30:53.956 align:middle
under money market rates and
keep the federal funds rate well

30:53.956 --> 30:56.736 align:middle
within its-well within
its range.

30:56.876 --> 30:58.146 align:middle
So we're not concerned with it.

30:58.146 --> 31:00.696 align:middle
It's doing-you have
an unusual situation

31:00.696 --> 31:04.996 align:middle
where the Treasury General
Account is, is shrinking

31:04.996 --> 31:06.356 align:middle
and bill supply is shrinking.

31:06.356 --> 31:08.236 align:middle
And so there's, there's
downward pressure-we're buying

31:08.236 --> 31:10.356 align:middle
assets-there's downward
pressure on short-term rates,

31:10.966 --> 31:14.286 align:middle
and that facility is, is doing
what we think it's supposed

31:14.286 --> 31:14.556 align:middle
to do.

31:15.196 --> 31:16.896 align:middle
Sorry, your second
question was again?

31:18.256 --> 31:18.926 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

31:18.926 --> 31:20.726 align:middle
Yeah, I mean, the change in
the rate control toolkit,

31:20.726 --> 31:23.796 align:middle
will that have any impact on-do
you think that will reduce the,

31:23.796 --> 31:25.506 align:middle
the amount of money
coming into reverse repos?

31:25.506 --> 31:28.646 align:middle
Will that have any impact on
money market conditions that,

31:28.646 --> 31:30.636 align:middle
you know, beyond the
fed funds rate setting?

31:30.706 --> 31:31.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:31.716 --> 31:32.936 align:middle
It could have some impact.

31:33.326 --> 31:35.016 align:middle
I think we'll have
to see empirically.

31:35.016 --> 31:38.806 align:middle
But it's designed to keep
the federal funds rate,

31:39.546 --> 31:41.606 align:middle
you know, within the range.

31:41.946 --> 31:45.976 align:middle
And I do think it could
have some effect on,

31:45.976 --> 31:48.256 align:middle
on broader money
market conditions below

31:48.256 --> 31:50.946 align:middle
as it relates to, you
know, the very low rates

31:50.946 --> 31:52.016 align:middle
and the downward pressures.

31:52.756 --> 31:53.826 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

31:53.826 --> 31:55.886 align:middle
And will it-do think
it will lower uptake

31:55.886 --> 31:57.246 align:middle
on the reverse repo facility,

31:57.246 --> 32:00.456 align:middle
or that's just not even really
a focus of what the change was?

32:00.456 --> 32:00.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:00.686 --> 32:01.666 align:middle
It's not, honestly.

32:01.666 --> 32:03.736 align:middle
And, you know, the funny thing,
you would think that it would,

32:04.296 --> 32:05.406 align:middle
but we'll have to see.

32:05.486 --> 32:09.176 align:middle
It's, it's possible that
that would not be the case.

32:09.176 --> 32:10.726 align:middle
That's going to be an
empirical question.

32:10.786 --> 32:12.246 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

32:12.246 --> 32:12.726 align:middle
Okay, cool.

32:12.726 --> 32:13.056 align:middle
Thank you.

32:13.056 --> 32:14.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:14.626 --> 32:16.866 align:middle
We'll go to Jeanna
Smialek, the New York Times.

32:19.366 --> 32:19.776 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

32:19.776 --> 32:20.046 align:middle
Hey, Chair Powell.

32:20.046 --> 32:21.696 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

32:21.696 --> 32:24.706 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could follow up a little bit

32:24.706 --> 32:27.316 align:middle
on your response to Rachel
at the very beginning

32:27.376 --> 32:31.326 align:middle
and talk a little bit about how
we should understand what full

32:31.326 --> 32:33.226 align:middle
employment means
in a world that,

32:33.226 --> 32:34.676 align:middle
as you mentioned,
is pretty roiled.

32:34.676 --> 32:37.146 align:middle
All the data is pretty-has been
pretty roiled by the pandemic,

32:37.146 --> 32:39.436 align:middle
and we're not really sure where
EPOP is going to settle in,

32:39.436 --> 32:40.276 align:middle
we're not sure

32:40.276 --> 32:41.806 align:middle
where participation
is going to settle in.

32:41.806 --> 32:44.436 align:middle
And wages are already
looking, you know, decent.

32:44.436 --> 32:46.596 align:middle
So I guess I wonder what
full employment means

32:46.596 --> 32:48.756 align:middle
in this context and, and
sort of how you're thinking

32:48.756 --> 32:53.116 align:middle
about those wage data.

32:53.296 --> 32:53.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:54.786 --> 32:56.206 align:middle
Yeah. As, as you well know,

32:56.966 --> 32:58.836 align:middle
there isn't one indicator
we can look to,

32:58.836 --> 33:01.226 align:middle
and there's no one number that
we can therefore point to.

33:01.226 --> 33:03.206 align:middle
We look at a range
of indicators,

33:03.206 --> 33:05.186 align:middle
and it's a very broad
range, you can count

33:05.186 --> 33:09.286 align:middle
to a high number just
quickly-but, but, certainly,

33:09.286 --> 33:12.696 align:middle
it will include things like
unemployment and participation

33:12.696 --> 33:16.896 align:middle
and wages and many
different flavors of that.

33:16.896 --> 33:20.026 align:middle
So how do we think about it?

33:20.766 --> 33:22.536 align:middle
A couple of things.

33:22.536 --> 33:24.726 align:middle
We're all going to be
informed by what we saw

33:24.726 --> 33:28.066 align:middle
in the last cycle, which was
labor supply outperforming

33:28.066 --> 33:30.116 align:middle
expectations over a
long period of time.

33:30.956 --> 33:33.986 align:middle
Now, that hadn't happened
in many other cycles,

33:33.986 --> 33:35.386 align:middle
but this was a very long cycle.

33:35.386 --> 33:38.106 align:middle
So we're going to have to
be alert to see whether

33:38.106 --> 33:39.076 align:middle
that can happen again.

33:39.486 --> 33:41.306 align:middle
It is a different-it's
a different economy.

33:41.306 --> 33:44.806 align:middle
We, we have had a
slew of retirements,

33:45.336 --> 33:47.916 align:middle
and that may weigh
on participation.

33:48.486 --> 33:50.396 align:middle
That, that effect,
though, should wear off

33:50.396 --> 33:52.376 align:middle
in a few years and,
and, you know,

33:52.716 --> 33:54.646 align:middle
as you move through that window,

33:55.236 --> 33:57.016 align:middle
because they would have-people
would have retired anyway,

33:57.136 --> 33:58.796 align:middle
and you'll be back where
you, you would have been.

33:59.276 --> 34:03.196 align:middle
So I think we're-I think
that lesson number one is,

34:03.196 --> 34:09.366 align:middle
is just to be careful about
assessing maximum employment.

34:10.076 --> 34:13.766 align:middle
And I think if you-during
the last cycle,

34:14.056 --> 34:15.716 align:middle
there were-there
were waves of concern

34:15.716 --> 34:18.566 align:middle
that we were reaching full
employment as early, you know,

34:18.566 --> 34:21.046 align:middle
as, as 2012, when I
arrived at the Fed.

34:21.596 --> 34:25.076 align:middle
And, you know-you know, nine
years later-eight years later,

34:25.956 --> 34:27.776 align:middle
we were still creating jobs.

34:27.776 --> 34:30.696 align:middle
And, you know, it
was quite remarkable.

34:30.696 --> 34:32.696 align:middle
So we're all going to
be informed by that.

34:32.696 --> 34:35.046 align:middle
At the same time, we understand
this is a different economy.

34:35.046 --> 34:38.896 align:middle
You know, the, the demographics
are, people are getting older,

34:38.896 --> 34:41.816 align:middle
and that should have
a secular effect of,

34:41.936 --> 34:45.356 align:middle
of reducing participation
over time.

34:45.676 --> 34:48.336 align:middle
So we have to be sensible
about what, what can be done.

34:48.336 --> 34:49.746 align:middle
But I think we're going
to be-we're going to lean

34:49.746 --> 34:50.806 align:middle
into that and be optimistic.

34:51.106 --> 34:52.126 align:middle
You asked about wages.

34:52.556 --> 34:54.486 align:middle
You know, we're seeing
wage increases.

34:54.486 --> 34:57.086 align:middle
That's, that's sort
of a natural thing

34:57.086 --> 34:58.516 align:middle
to be seeing in a
strong economy.

34:59.206 --> 35:01.926 align:middle
And what we're seeing is-we
don't see anything that's

35:01.926 --> 35:04.566 align:middle
troubling in the sense of-what
would be troubling would be,

35:05.046 --> 35:08.746 align:middle
you know, very wide
across the economy,

35:08.746 --> 35:12.596 align:middle
wages at unsustainable levels
without high inflation.

35:12.596 --> 35:15.746 align:middle
In other words, wages in excess
of productivity and inflation,

35:15.746 --> 35:19.866 align:middle
you know, by a meaningful amount
broadly across the economy,

35:20.326 --> 35:23.416 align:middle
sort of forcing companies to
keep raising prices and getting

35:23.416 --> 35:25.086 align:middle
into a wage-price cycle.

35:25.086 --> 35:28.076 align:middle
That's, that's the old formula
for-one of the old formulas

35:28.076 --> 35:29.566 align:middle
for having high inflation.

35:29.566 --> 35:30.946 align:middle
We don't see anything
like that now.

35:30.946 --> 35:32.356 align:middle
We do see high wages.

35:32.776 --> 35:36.216 align:middle
We see them for, for people
who are mostly new, you know,

35:36.216 --> 35:39.296 align:middle
entering into new jobs, many
of them in low-skilled jobs.

35:39.776 --> 35:43.816 align:middle
And, but we, we do think-you've
got to-you've got to think

35:43.876 --> 35:46.106 align:middle
in the labor market right
now, where, where supply

35:46.106 --> 35:48.316 align:middle
and demand are just
not matched up well.

35:48.816 --> 35:51.056 align:middle
And, you know, we think
it's a flexible economy and,

35:51.056 --> 35:51.926 align:middle
and it will clear.

35:51.926 --> 35:55.766 align:middle
There will-there will be a level
at which supply and demand meet.

35:56.296 --> 35:58.016 align:middle
And that'll-we think that'll,

35:58.446 --> 35:59.976 align:middle
that'll be happening
in coming months.

35:59.976 --> 36:04.396 align:middle
So-but the last thing I'll
say is, again, if you look at,

36:04.396 --> 36:06.956 align:middle
at the forecasts,
we are going to be

36:07.266 --> 36:12.226 align:middle
in a very strong labor
market pretty quickly here.

36:12.226 --> 36:14.686 align:middle
There are still a big
group of unemployed people.

36:15.356 --> 36:17.306 align:middle
And, you know, we're not
going to forget about them.

36:17.306 --> 36:19.326 align:middle
We're going-we're going
to do everything we can

36:19.326 --> 36:21.986 align:middle
to get people back into work and
give them the chance to work.

36:22.686 --> 36:25.746 align:middle
But there's every reason
to think that we'll be

36:25.746 --> 36:28.926 align:middle
in a-in a labor market with
very attractive numbers,

36:28.926 --> 36:31.066 align:middle
with low unemployment,
high participation,

36:31.456 --> 36:33.256 align:middle
and rising wages
across the spectrum.

36:33.256 --> 36:36.766 align:middle
So that's, that's a little
bit how we're looking

36:36.766 --> 36:37.826 align:middle
at the-at the labor market.

36:37.826 --> 36:38.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:40.926 --> 36:41.606 align:middle
Thank you.

36:41.606 --> 36:43.976 align:middle
Now we'll go to Hannah Lang
at the American Banker.

36:43.976 --> 36:45.586 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

36:45.586 --> 36:48.536 align:middle
Hi, I wanted to ask about
the status of your thinking

36:48.536 --> 36:51.136 align:middle
around the supplementary
leverage ratio right now.

36:51.376 --> 36:54.716 align:middle
Is the Fed still thinking about
ways to permanently adjust this

36:54.716 --> 36:56.506 align:middle
to account for the high
growth in deposits?

36:56.506 --> 36:59.816 align:middle
And do you ultimately believe
a permanent fix is needed?

37:00.536 --> 37:02.546 align:middle
And any information
on the timing

37:02.546 --> 37:05.006 align:middle
around that would
be-would be helpful.

37:05.056 --> 37:05.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:05.396 --> 37:07.786 align:middle
What I can say is,
we're working on it.

37:07.916 --> 37:10.086 align:middle
I don't have anything to
share with you in terms

37:10.086 --> 37:12.986 align:middle
of the particulars or the
timing right now, unfortunately.

37:13.606 --> 37:16.236 align:middle
But we've, we've
always-our position has been

37:16.236 --> 37:18.506 align:middle
for a long time and,
and it is now that we'd,

37:18.506 --> 37:20.986 align:middle
we'd like the leverage
ratio to be a backstop

37:20.986 --> 37:22.546 align:middle
to risk-based capital
requirements.

37:23.306 --> 37:26.156 align:middle
When leverage requirements
are, are, are binding,

37:26.536 --> 37:28.036 align:middle
it does skew incentives
for firms

37:28.036 --> 37:31.076 align:middle
to substitute lower-risk
assets for high-risk ones.

37:31.236 --> 37:32.286 align:middle
It's a straightforward thing.

37:32.996 --> 37:35.476 align:middle
And because of the substantial
increase in reserves,

37:35.476 --> 37:37.736 align:middle
Treasuries, and other safe
assets in the banking system,

37:37.736 --> 37:40.836 align:middle
the SLR is rapidly
ceasing to become-ceasing

37:40.836 --> 37:44.866 align:middle
to be the intended backstop for
big firms that we want it to be.

37:44.986 --> 37:49.396 align:middle
So we do think it's appropriate
to consider ways to adapt it

37:49.686 --> 37:51.746 align:middle
to this new high-reserves
environment, and,

37:51.746 --> 37:52.996 align:middle
and we're looking
hard at the issue.

37:53.656 --> 37:55.946 align:middle
We would also, just
to be really clear,

37:56.196 --> 37:59.556 align:middle
we will take whatever actions
are necessary to assure

37:59.556 --> 38:01.286 align:middle
that any changes we do make

38:01.496 --> 38:04.226 align:middle
or recommend do not erode
the overall strength

38:04.726 --> 38:06.046 align:middle
of bank capital requirements.

38:08.466 --> 38:09.836 align:middle
Sorry, I can't give
you any more.

38:10.016 --> 38:12.656 align:middle
That's just something
we're working on.

38:13.056 --> 38:13.856 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:14.196 --> 38:14.836 align:middle
Thank you.

38:14.836 --> 38:18.486 align:middle
Now we'll go to Anneken
Tappe at CNN Business.

38:18.486 --> 38:20.866 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

38:21.066 --> 38:22.576 align:middle
Hi there. Thanks for
taking my question.

38:23.106 --> 38:25.776 align:middle
Chairman Powell, the
price jumps we've seen

38:25.776 --> 38:28.296 align:middle
in some raw materials-lumber,
for example, you mentioned

38:28.296 --> 38:30.316 align:middle
that earlier-seem to be easing.

38:30.316 --> 38:31.776 align:middle
And it looks like
we're at the beginning

38:31.776 --> 38:33.776 align:middle
of suppliers catching
up with demand.

38:33.776 --> 38:36.926 align:middle
But I wonder if you're worried
at all, if we're going to end

38:36.926 --> 38:38.806 align:middle
up with excess supply
immediately

38:38.806 --> 38:40.276 align:middle
after those shortages wear off

38:40.276 --> 38:44.596 align:middle
and if we just continue this
mismatch, as we're recovering

38:44.596 --> 38:47.166 align:middle
and getting out of
the pandemic economy.

38:47.166 --> 38:50.076 align:middle
And I wonder how that would
affect the Fed's outlook at all.

38:50.076 --> 38:51.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:51.586 --> 38:53.656 align:middle
Well, that is really not the
problem we're having right now.

38:53.736 --> 38:55.626 align:middle
But-and, actually,
people who work

38:55.626 --> 38:58.346 align:middle
in commodity industries
are very focused on that,

38:58.346 --> 39:01.516 align:middle
because they know that, you
know, they don't want to build,

39:02.076 --> 39:04.616 align:middle
build capacity and then find
out that it's not necessary.

39:05.856 --> 39:07.636 align:middle
So, really, the problem now is,

39:07.716 --> 39:11.166 align:middle
is that demand is
very, very strong.

39:11.166 --> 39:14.736 align:middle
Incomes are high, people have
money in their bank accounts.

39:15.556 --> 39:17.986 align:middle
Demand for goods
is extremely high,

39:17.986 --> 39:20.826 align:middle
and it hasn't-it
hasn't come down.

39:21.166 --> 39:23.866 align:middle
We're seeing the
service sector reopening.

39:23.866 --> 39:26.356 align:middle
And so you're seeing
prices are moving back

39:26.356 --> 39:27.596 align:middle
up off their lows there.

39:28.156 --> 39:31.126 align:middle
But in terms of, of
overcorrecting, I mean,

39:31.126 --> 39:33.746 align:middle
I think there, there is a
possibility on the other side

39:33.746 --> 39:37.506 align:middle
of this that, that inflation
could be-could actually be quite

39:37.506 --> 39:38.926 align:middle
low going forward.

39:38.926 --> 39:41.546 align:middle
But that's not-that's not really
where our focus is right now.

39:41.546 --> 39:47.666 align:middle
Our focus right now is, we
need to-our, our expectation is

39:47.666 --> 39:51.386 align:middle
that these, these high
inflation readings

39:51.426 --> 39:54.326 align:middle
that we're seeing now
will start to abate.

39:54.436 --> 39:55.616 align:middle
And that's, that's
what we think.

39:55.616 --> 39:58.136 align:middle
And it'll be like the
lumber experience,

39:58.176 --> 40:00.566 align:middle
and like we expect the
used car experience to be.

40:00.856 --> 40:04.696 align:middle
With things like airplane
tickets and hotels,

40:04.696 --> 40:07.776 align:middle
which are the other two factors
in the most recent CPI report

40:07.776 --> 40:10.826 align:middle
that went up a lot, we expect
that those prices will get back

40:10.826 --> 40:12.896 align:middle
up to where they were, but
there's no reason to think

40:12.896 --> 40:14.346 align:middle
that they're going to
keep going up a lot.

40:15.636 --> 40:18.116 align:middle
Because if they are, people
will build new hotels.

40:18.116 --> 40:20.476 align:middle
There's no reason for supply
and demand to be out of whack

40:20.476 --> 40:22.336 align:middle
in the hotel business
over any period of time.

40:22.336 --> 40:23.946 align:middle
So we think that'll happen.

40:24.416 --> 40:28.756 align:middle
I think in terms of the timing
and the effects on inflation

40:28.756 --> 40:30.766 align:middle
in the near term, there's
a lot of uncertainty.

40:31.176 --> 40:34.226 align:middle
The overall story is one
that, that we think is right,

40:34.226 --> 40:38.956 align:middle
and we think the incoming
data support it and, you know,

40:38.956 --> 40:40.946 align:middle
so do many, many forecasters.

40:40.946 --> 40:43.856 align:middle
And if you look at the
forecasts on the FOMC,

40:43.856 --> 40:46.106 align:middle
you will-you will
see that as well.

40:46.476 --> 40:52.366 align:middle
But we don't-we don't in
any way dismiss the chance

40:52.546 --> 40:54.566 align:middle
that it can work out
that, that this goes

40:54.566 --> 40:55.806 align:middle
on longer than expected.

40:55.806 --> 40:58.586 align:middle
And the risk would be that
over time, it does begin

40:58.586 --> 41:00.286 align:middle
to affect inflation
expectations.

41:00.726 --> 41:02.646 align:middle
And if we see inflation
expectations

41:03.186 --> 41:06.316 align:middle
and inflation-or
inflation moving up in a way

41:06.316 --> 41:10.916 align:middle
that is really materially
above what we-what we would see

41:10.916 --> 41:14.586 align:middle
as consistent with our
goals, and persistently so,

41:14.586 --> 41:17.806 align:middle
we wouldn't hesitate to use
our tools to address that.

41:18.236 --> 41:20.036 align:middle
Price stability is
half of our mandate,

41:20.036 --> 41:22.406 align:middle
and we would certainly do that.

41:22.716 --> 41:24.026 align:middle
We do not expect that, though.

41:24.026 --> 41:25.326 align:middle
That is not our base case.

41:25.326 --> 41:29.116 align:middle
And, and in that we're joined
by many other forecasters,

41:29.116 --> 41:32.286 align:middle
but there's a lot to be humble
about among forecasters.

41:32.286 --> 41:33.826 align:middle
Forecasters have a lot
to be humble about.

41:33.826 --> 41:35.826 align:middle
It's a-it's a highly
uncertain business.

41:36.476 --> 41:39.596 align:middle
And we're, we're very much
attuned to the risks and,

41:39.596 --> 41:41.116 align:middle
and watching the data carefully.

41:41.606 --> 41:44.016 align:middle
In the meantime, I
would say, you know,

41:44.016 --> 41:45.946 align:middle
we should-as I mentioned
earlier,

41:46.226 --> 41:48.276 align:middle
there's so much uncertainty
around this.

41:48.276 --> 41:51.816 align:middle
It's, it's just a unique
situation that we need

41:51.816 --> 41:54.346 align:middle
to see how things evolve
in coming months and,

41:54.346 --> 41:58.366 align:middle
and see how that story holds
up and act accordingly.

41:58.366 --> 41:59.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:02.326 --> 42:02.986 align:middle
Thank you.

42:02.986 --> 42:04.806 align:middle
We'll go to Howard
Schneider at Reuters.

42:05.296 --> 42:06.526 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

42:06.736 --> 42:07.806 align:middle
Howard Schneider, Reuters.

42:07.806 --> 42:09.096 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell,
for taking this.

42:09.096 --> 42:10.536 align:middle
I don't want to miss
the moment here.

42:10.536 --> 42:12.786 align:middle
And I just-I noticed that in
the statement you dropped the

42:12.786 --> 42:15.726 align:middle
language saying that
the pandemic is weighing

42:15.726 --> 42:17.056 align:middle
on the economy.

42:17.056 --> 42:21.466 align:middle
So is this the effective end,
in your view, of the pandemic

42:21.466 --> 42:23.666 align:middle
as a constraint on
economic activity,

42:23.746 --> 42:25.386 align:middle
even though it's
still cited as a risk?

42:25.386 --> 42:27.356 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:27.536 --> 42:30.206 align:middle
You know, it's a-it's
a continuum, right?

42:30.386 --> 42:35.736 align:middle
What you've seen with the
pandemic is sharply declining

42:36.206 --> 42:38.256 align:middle
cases, hospitalizations,
and deaths.

42:38.256 --> 42:38.946 align:middle
And that's great.

42:39.706 --> 42:42.556 align:middle
And, and, you know,
that should continue.

42:42.626 --> 42:45.266 align:middle
But, you know, you, you also
saw in the United Kingdom,

42:45.266 --> 42:47.076 align:middle
which has, I think,
at least as high

42:47.076 --> 42:48.526 align:middle
if not higher vaccination rates,

42:48.606 --> 42:52.366 align:middle
they've had an outbreak
of the Delta variety.

42:52.366 --> 42:54.146 align:middle
And it's-and it's causing them

42:54.146 --> 42:56.926 align:middle
to have to-to have
to react to that.

42:56.926 --> 42:59.586 align:middle
So you're not-you're not out
of the woods at this point.

42:59.586 --> 43:02.316 align:middle
And it would be premature
to-in my thinking,

43:02.316 --> 43:04.576 align:middle
it would be premature
to declare victory.

43:05.236 --> 43:08.256 align:middle
Vaccination still has
a ways to go to get

43:08.256 --> 43:10.696 align:middle
to levels-it would
be good to see it get

43:10.696 --> 43:12.156 align:middle
to a substantially higher level.

43:12.786 --> 43:15.216 align:middle
And, you know, that
can only help.

43:16.196 --> 43:17.746 align:middle
So, look, I-but you're right,

43:17.746 --> 43:19.296 align:middle
the statement language
is evolving.

43:19.296 --> 43:21.086 align:middle
I would expect it to
continue to evolve.

43:21.696 --> 43:23.436 align:middle
There's a lot of
judgment in that.

43:23.436 --> 43:26.326 align:middle
But you can expect us to drag
our feet a little bit on that,

43:26.326 --> 43:29.596 align:middle
because that's what you do
with statement language.

43:29.596 --> 43:31.626 align:middle
It's, it's great to
see the progress.

43:31.666 --> 43:33.706 align:middle
But, again, I would not
declare victory yet.

43:33.706 --> 43:36.916 align:middle
I would say it is so
great to see the reopening

43:36.916 --> 43:38.976 align:middle
of the economy, though,
and to see people

43:38.976 --> 43:40.516 align:middle
out living their lives again.

43:40.646 --> 43:42.216 align:middle
You know, who doesn't
want to see that?

43:42.786 --> 43:45.936 align:middle
And it appears to be safe, and
I just would encourage people

43:45.936 --> 43:47.156 align:middle
to continue to get vaccinated.

43:47.216 --> 43:47.766 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

43:48.436 --> 43:50.106 align:middle
If I could follow up on that,

43:50.106 --> 43:52.376 align:middle
if you-if you view this
statement in, in toto

43:52.376 --> 43:55.396 align:middle
and the-and the dots and
the substance as well,

43:55.396 --> 43:59.586 align:middle
do you think this is more
a mark-to-market exercise

43:59.586 --> 44:01.706 align:middle
around the improvement in health

44:01.706 --> 44:04.546 align:middle
or around the inflation risks
you see developing out there?

44:04.746 --> 44:05.116 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:05.116 --> 44:05.746 align:middle
I think it's both.

44:06.016 --> 44:12.666 align:middle
You know, I think, clearly,
since March what's happened is,

44:13.326 --> 44:19.286 align:middle
people have grown more confident
in these very strong outcomes,

44:19.286 --> 44:20.186 align:middle
that they'll be achieved.

44:20.186 --> 44:22.356 align:middle
Very strong outcomes in the
economy will be achieved.

44:22.786 --> 44:24.226 align:middle
There's, there's more
grounds for comfort.

44:24.226 --> 44:26.296 align:middle
We've seen growth coming
higher than we expected.

44:26.566 --> 44:28.366 align:middle
We've seen very strong
labor demand.

44:29.026 --> 44:32.666 align:middle
We've also seen-we have seen
inflation above target, though,

44:32.666 --> 44:34.936 align:middle
and I think even
though, you know, in,

44:34.936 --> 44:39.176 align:middle
in our forecasters' case, they
do see inflation coming back

44:39.176 --> 44:43.816 align:middle
down over '22 and '23 into, into
areas that are very consistent

44:43.816 --> 44:45.676 align:middle
with our-with our mandate.

44:45.676 --> 44:48.356 align:middle
Nonetheless, the risk is,
is something that can factor

44:48.356 --> 44:51.286 align:middle
into people's thinking about
appropriate monetary policy.

44:51.606 --> 44:54.636 align:middle
The thing is, you know, these
are 18 different forecasts,

44:54.636 --> 44:58.766 align:middle
and I can't stand here
and say exactly what was

44:58.766 --> 44:59.996 align:middle
in all 18 people's minds.

44:59.996 --> 45:02.596 align:middle
But that, that is something that
I think can factor into things

45:02.596 --> 45:05.466 align:middle
as well-factor into
our forecast as well.

45:05.986 --> 45:07.026 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

45:07.026 --> 45:07.406 align:middle
Thank you.

45:07.406 --> 45:07.966 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:07.966 --> 45:09.026 align:middle
Thank you.

45:09.026 --> 45:10.996 align:middle
We'll go to Victoria
Guida with Politico.

45:10.996 --> 45:14.426 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

45:14.516 --> 45:15.256 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

45:15.256 --> 45:17.956 align:middle
I, I wanted to ask a little
bit more about inflation

45:17.956 --> 45:20.616 align:middle
to make sure I understand how
you're thinking about this.

45:20.616 --> 45:24.856 align:middle
So in the projections,
inflation is expected to,

45:24.856 --> 45:27.576 align:middle
to be high this year and
then come back down next year

45:27.966 --> 45:31.296 align:middle
and then maybe start to
rise a little bit again,

45:31.296 --> 45:33.996 align:middle
enough for a liftoff in 2023.

45:34.096 --> 45:35.546 align:middle
You know, I know that's
obviously-you know,

45:35.546 --> 45:37.476 align:middle
take that with a grain of salt.

45:37.476 --> 45:41.886 align:middle
But that would suggest that
you all could theoretically see

45:41.886 --> 45:45.486 align:middle
inflation sustainably
staying above 2 percent.

45:45.486 --> 45:47.196 align:middle
And so, I guess my question is,

45:47.726 --> 45:49.936 align:middle
what would be causing
that inflation?

45:49.936 --> 45:53.106 align:middle
What, what would
be-because it seems

45:53.106 --> 45:54.956 align:middle
like you all now see a situation

45:54.956 --> 45:56.786 align:middle
in which inflation
would be rising in a way

45:56.786 --> 45:58.866 align:middle
that isn't caused by
transitory factors

45:59.326 --> 46:01.136 align:middle
in the-in the next
couple of years.

46:01.136 --> 46:03.396 align:middle
So would that be the result
of a tight labor market?

46:03.396 --> 46:06.106 align:middle
Would that be because this whole
situation has raised people's

46:06.106 --> 46:07.266 align:middle
inflation expectations?

46:07.266 --> 46:08.396 align:middle
How are you thinking about that?

46:08.396 --> 46:08.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:08.616 --> 46:11.786 align:middle
So what we're seeing in the near
term-again, our base case is

46:11.786 --> 46:13.186 align:middle
that what we're seeing
in the near term is,

46:13.266 --> 46:17.196 align:middle
is principally associated
with, with the reopening

46:17.196 --> 46:19.566 align:middle
of the economy and not
with a tight labor market

46:19.566 --> 46:21.436 align:middle
or tight resource
constraints, really.

46:22.276 --> 46:23.236 align:middle
So-but you're right.

46:23.266 --> 46:28.066 align:middle
When, when you get to-in, in the
forecast, all of that, you know,

46:28.066 --> 46:30.366 align:middle
supply and demand sides
of the economy adapt.

46:30.366 --> 46:32.336 align:middle
We have a very highly
adaptive, you know,

46:32.336 --> 46:34.406 align:middle
flexible economy,
more so than most.

46:34.916 --> 46:41.086 align:middle
And by 2023, those increases are
really about, about, you know,

46:41.226 --> 46:44.146 align:middle
rising resource utilization
or, to put it a different way,

46:44.146 --> 46:45.346 align:middle
you know, low unemployment,

46:45.976 --> 46:48.096 align:middle
or high employment is a
way to think about it.

46:48.096 --> 46:49.516 align:middle
So that's what that's about.

46:49.516 --> 46:53.596 align:middle
That's about the kind of
broad inflationary pressure

46:53.596 --> 46:55.796 align:middle
that results from, you know,

46:55.866 --> 46:57.936 align:middle
a really strong expansion
tightening

46:57.936 --> 47:00.216 align:middle
up resource utilization
across the whole economy

47:00.216 --> 47:02.506 align:middle
and lifting, lifting
up inflation.

47:02.576 --> 47:05.166 align:middle
And that's why-that's why
you would see it then,

47:05.166 --> 47:07.026 align:middle
because by then, you know,

47:07.026 --> 47:09.226 align:middle
in the forecast-and
it's just a forecast,

47:09.876 --> 47:11.556 align:middle
they're just individual
forecasts.

47:11.556 --> 47:14.736 align:middle
In people's forecasts,
that's what's happening.

47:16.076 --> 47:17.156 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

47:17.156 --> 47:19.666 align:middle
So the, the, the change in the
projections reflect the fact

47:19.666 --> 47:22.936 align:middle
that you all are more optimistic
about the economic outlook,

47:22.936 --> 47:24.246 align:middle
and not necessarily
that you think

47:24.246 --> 47:26.276 align:middle
that this will change the way
people think about inflation?

47:27.816 --> 47:28.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:28.696 --> 47:31.366 align:middle
Yeah, I think-there may be an
element of the latter as well,

47:31.366 --> 47:33.836 align:middle
because inflation expectations
have continued to move up.

47:34.566 --> 47:36.976 align:middle
You know, it's all in
people's individual thinking,

47:37.126 --> 47:38.846 align:middle
and you can't-it's hard to say.

47:38.846 --> 47:42.206 align:middle
It's not something the Committee
debates in terms of, you know,

47:42.206 --> 47:44.446 align:middle
what, what the outlook
is for 2023.

47:44.446 --> 47:46.576 align:middle
So I'm, I'm a little
bit speculating,

47:46.576 --> 47:47.316 align:middle
which I shouldn't do.

47:47.316 --> 47:49.336 align:middle
But it wouldn't surprise
me if there's an element

47:49.336 --> 47:52.976 align:middle
for some people in, you know,
seeing the inflation performance

47:52.976 --> 47:56.526 align:middle
that we've had and thinking
that I have more confidence

47:56.526 --> 47:58.636 align:middle
that we could see
inflation above 2 percent,

47:59.426 --> 48:01.986 align:middle
that it may not be as hard
to do that as we thought,

48:02.366 --> 48:04.796 align:middle
and that inflation
expectations may move

48:04.796 --> 48:08.046 align:middle
up to a-to a level-they
were-they were really at a level

48:08.046 --> 48:11.116 align:middle
that was kind of a
little below 2 percent.

48:11.596 --> 48:14.316 align:middle
They might move up as a
consequence of this or, or,

48:14.516 --> 48:17.616 align:middle
or as a consequence of,
of, of the new framework.

48:17.616 --> 48:20.216 align:middle
You know, we did see
inflation expectations moving

48:21.056 --> 48:23.686 align:middle
up in the-in the wake of the
announcement of the framework.

48:23.726 --> 48:25.486 align:middle
But, you know, we
don't really know that.

48:25.486 --> 48:29.246 align:middle
So, ultimately, I
think it's consistent

48:29.246 --> 48:30.006 align:middle
with both those things.

48:30.006 --> 48:30.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:33.176 --> 48:33.586 align:middle
Thank you.

48:33.586 --> 48:36.026 align:middle
Now we'll go to Greg
Robb at MarketWatch.

48:37.326 --> 48:37.836 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

48:37.836 --> 48:39.666 align:middle
Hi. Hi, thank you for
taking my question.

48:40.256 --> 48:42.186 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'm just
looking at the forecast,

48:42.186 --> 48:44.866 align:middle
and one thing I just don't
think has been talked about all

48:44.866 --> 48:47.926 align:middle
that much is how much
you guys-the Fed thinks

48:47.926 --> 48:50.496 align:middle
that the economy is
going to slow next year.

48:50.496 --> 48:52.656 align:middle
I mean, are we looking
at a scenario

48:52.656 --> 48:57.336 align:middle
of a slowing economy next
year with higher inflation?

48:58.126 --> 48:59.356 align:middle
And, and what do you
think about that?

48:59.356 --> 49:00.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:00.136 --> 49:01.436 align:middle
We're looking at an economy

49:01.436 --> 49:04.666 align:middle
that will not have the
degree of, of fiscal support.

49:04.956 --> 49:09.176 align:middle
The fiscal support in
the forecast is much less

49:09.176 --> 49:10.026 align:middle
than it was this year.

49:10.026 --> 49:13.446 align:middle
So-but you've, you've still
got a very strong growth,

49:13.446 --> 49:16.926 align:middle
well above the longer-run
potential output

49:16.926 --> 49:17.986 align:middle
of the-of the economy.

49:17.986 --> 49:20.836 align:middle
You've got-you've got growth
meaningfully above that,

49:20.836 --> 49:23.426 align:middle
and inflation is
lower next year in all

49:23.426 --> 49:24.866 align:middle
of our-in all of our forecasts.

49:24.866 --> 49:27.756 align:middle
I think the range of,
of core PCE forecast

49:27.756 --> 49:35.456 align:middle
for next year is 1.7 to 2.5 in
2022 and, and 2 to 2.3 in 2023.

49:35.926 --> 49:39.156 align:middle
So you're right,
you're seeing-I,

49:39.486 --> 49:42.956 align:middle
I can't remember the number,
but it might be in the 3s-3,

49:42.956 --> 49:44.956 align:middle
3½ percent growth
for next year.

49:45.596 --> 49:48.396 align:middle
That's, that's a really
good year-coming on the back

49:48.396 --> 49:51.636 align:middle
of a 7 percent growth year,
that's a really good year.

49:51.636 --> 49:53.346 align:middle
That's, that's a year
with a lot of momentum.

49:53.346 --> 49:57.916 align:middle
That'll see-you know, that'll
cause significant job creation

49:58.486 --> 50:02.966 align:middle
and, and it will-I mean,
we would take 3½ percent.

50:04.206 --> 50:06.976 align:middle
We didn't have a 3½ percent
growth year-we didn't have a 3

50:06.976 --> 50:09.746 align:middle
percent growth year between
the Global Financial Crisis

50:09.746 --> 50:10.846 align:middle
and the end of the expansion.

50:11.626 --> 50:12.536 align:middle
So that would be a good year.

50:12.536 --> 50:13.236 align:middle
GREGG ROBB.

50:15.316 --> 50:19.466 align:middle
Doesn't it seem like there's
a risk of, of, you know,

50:19.466 --> 50:22.226 align:middle
like stagflation-where
that-you're going to go

50:22.226 --> 50:25.426 align:middle
from 7 percent and down, that
means the economy's really,

50:25.666 --> 50:27.696 align:middle
you know, dropping in some way.

50:27.696 --> 50:28.686 align:middle
We haven't seen that, right?

50:28.686 --> 50:29.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:29.096 --> 50:30.406 align:middle
Well, the economy's not,

50:30.406 --> 50:34.576 align:middle
not decel-the economy is still
growing, and growing at a,

50:34.576 --> 50:36.266 align:middle
at a very healthy rate.

50:36.266 --> 50:38.226 align:middle
Our estimate-I mean,
different people have different

50:38.226 --> 50:40.416 align:middle
estimates-but, broadly speaking,

50:40.416 --> 50:42.546 align:middle
economists think the economy
has the potential to grow

50:42.546 --> 50:43.896 align:middle
at around 2 percent per year.

50:43.896 --> 50:45.456 align:middle
If you're growing above that,

50:46.126 --> 50:48.316 align:middle
then the unemployment
rate should be declining,

50:48.316 --> 50:50.116 align:middle
people should be being
pulled into the labor force,

50:50.116 --> 50:52.676 align:middle
wages should be going up, lots
of things should be happening,

50:52.806 --> 50:54.006 align:middle
businesses should be investing.

50:54.006 --> 50:58.746 align:middle
So, you know, I guess to answer
your question a different way,

50:58.746 --> 51:01.566 align:middle
is there a risk that inflation
will be higher than we think?

51:01.566 --> 51:04.166 align:middle
Yes. As I said earlier,
you know, we,

51:04.166 --> 51:08.266 align:middle
we don't have any certainty
about the timing or the extent

51:08.266 --> 51:10.186 align:middle
of these effects from reopening.

51:10.306 --> 51:13.666 align:middle
And therefore we don't-we
don't think that-we think it's

51:13.666 --> 51:18.266 align:middle
unlikely that they would
materially affect the underlying

51:18.266 --> 51:20.236 align:middle
inflation dynamics that
the economy has had

51:20.236 --> 51:21.356 align:middle
for a quarter of a century.

51:21.856 --> 51:24.816 align:middle
The underlying forces
around the globe

51:25.226 --> 51:27.446 align:middle
that have created those
dynamics are intact,

51:27.446 --> 51:30.986 align:middle
and those are aging population,
low productivity, globalization,

51:31.556 --> 51:35.786 align:middle
all of those things that, that
we think have, have, you know,

51:35.786 --> 51:37.146 align:middle
really held down inflation.

51:37.146 --> 51:38.306 align:middle
All that's out there still.

51:38.406 --> 51:39.586 align:middle
You know, when we
get through this,

51:39.946 --> 51:41.896 align:middle
we may well-we'll be
facing those same forces.

51:42.106 --> 51:44.386 align:middle
Nonetheless, is there a risk

51:44.386 --> 51:46.486 align:middle
that inflation will remain
higher than we-than we thought?

51:46.486 --> 51:51.246 align:middle
Yes. And if, if we see inflation
moving above our goals in,

51:51.246 --> 51:55.596 align:middle
in a time-sorry, to an
extent, to a level or,

51:55.596 --> 51:59.676 align:middle
or persistently-or
persistently enough, you know,

51:59.676 --> 52:02.526 align:middle
we would be prepared to use
our tools to address that.

52:02.636 --> 52:03.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

52:05.926 --> 52:06.296 align:middle
Thank you.

52:06.296 --> 52:07.686 align:middle
Going to Brian Cheung
with Yahoo.

52:07.686 --> 52:08.416 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

52:09.016 --> 52:11.136 align:middle
Chair Powell, Brian
Cheung, Yahoo Finance.

52:11.136 --> 52:13.216 align:middle
On that point, you
talked about maybe some

52:13.216 --> 52:14.926 align:middle
of the more structural
changes in that last answer

52:14.926 --> 52:16.386 align:middle
with regards to productivity.

52:16.386 --> 52:19.576 align:middle
I noted that the median
projection for r*,

52:19.576 --> 52:21.356 align:middle
the longer-term interest rate,

52:21.636 --> 52:23.446 align:middle
is still the same
at 2.5 percent.

52:23.446 --> 52:25.346 align:middle
But there's been some
literature out there

52:25.346 --> 52:28.206 align:middle
that maybe the COVID crisis
could have actually changed some

52:28.206 --> 52:30.586 align:middle
of the underlying
fundamentals of the economy

52:30.586 --> 52:33.356 align:middle
and maybe changed productivity,
in addition to combined

52:33.356 --> 52:36.006 align:middle
with demographic changes that
have already been in effect

52:36.006 --> 52:38.886 align:middle
to suggest that that
longer-term neutral rate

52:38.886 --> 52:40.226 align:middle
or r* might be higher.

52:40.446 --> 52:43.646 align:middle
What would the implications of
that be for monetary policy?

52:43.646 --> 52:46.636 align:middle
Do you think that maybe the
Fed could have the possibility

52:46.636 --> 52:49.566 align:middle
of underestimating the
long-run neutral rate?

52:49.766 --> 52:51.086 align:middle
And what might be
the impact of that?

52:52.166 --> 52:52.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:52.716 --> 52:55.336 align:middle
A higher neutral rate would mean

52:55.336 --> 52:58.136 align:middle
that interest rates would
run higher by that amount.

52:58.136 --> 53:02.426 align:middle
And, and that would be a good
thing from the standpoint

53:02.976 --> 53:05.926 align:middle
of the economy, because it
would give the Fed more room

53:05.926 --> 53:06.696 align:middle
to cut rates.

53:07.336 --> 53:09.576 align:middle
The problem with, with
interest rates being close

53:09.576 --> 53:12.206 align:middle
to the lower bound, of
course, is that it really cuts

53:12.206 --> 53:13.456 align:middle
into our ability to react

53:13.456 --> 53:15.546 align:middle
to a downturn-for
example, a pandemic.

53:15.936 --> 53:19.106 align:middle
And if you look, for example,
at the European Central Bank,

53:19.146 --> 53:22.256 align:middle
their, their policy rate
was well below zero when,

53:23.016 --> 53:23.906 align:middle
when the pandemic hit.

53:23.906 --> 53:25.856 align:middle
So we don't want to be in a
place where we can't react.

53:25.856 --> 53:28.746 align:middle
A higher neutral rate
would, would be-from

53:28.746 --> 53:31.426 align:middle
that narrow standpoint,
would be a good thing for us.

53:31.426 --> 53:33.486 align:middle
It would give us more room and,
therefore, then, that would tend

53:33.486 --> 53:36.156 align:middle
to result in better outcomes
for the economy over time.

53:36.906 --> 53:39.766 align:middle
You know, we, it's-you
can't estimate it

53:39.766 --> 53:41.386 align:middle
with great-with great precision.

53:42.066 --> 53:44.716 align:middle
I think we would
be alert to-I mean,

53:44.806 --> 53:47.706 align:middle
studying r* is a-is a
whole industry unto itself.

53:47.706 --> 53:52.356 align:middle
And I, I think we would be alert
to factors that might raise r*,

53:52.356 --> 53:55.216 align:middle
the neutral rate of interest.

53:55.776 --> 53:57.676 align:middle
And, you know, we, we
try to keep up with that.

53:57.676 --> 53:59.796 align:middle
And I think we're, we're,
we're all thinking about that

53:59.796 --> 54:03.346 align:middle
and the possibility of that.

54:03.346 --> 54:06.366 align:middle
You know, there are many-there
are a lot of stories right now

54:06.416 --> 54:09.466 align:middle
that could-that essentially
could lead

54:09.466 --> 54:12.076 align:middle
to higher productivity
growth and higher r*.

54:12.666 --> 54:14.376 align:middle
We don't know which of those
stories will come true.

54:14.376 --> 54:15.966 align:middle
But, I mean, I'll
give you an example.

54:15.966 --> 54:17.646 align:middle
It's just there are-there
are a lot of start-ups,

54:18.306 --> 54:19.806 align:middle
a lot of early-stage companies.

54:19.896 --> 54:23.246 align:middle
And is that going
to have that effect?

54:23.246 --> 54:23.796 align:middle
We don't know.

54:23.986 --> 54:25.616 align:middle
But we'll be watching
those things carefully.

54:25.756 --> 54:26.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:29.016 --> 54:29.836 align:middle
Great, thank you.

54:29.836 --> 54:30.976 align:middle
For the last question, we'll go

54:30.976 --> 54:32.826 align:middle
to Michael McKee
at Bloomberg TV.

54:33.046 --> 54:35.446 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

54:35.446 --> 54:38.326 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, of course
you'll be shocked to learn

54:38.326 --> 54:40.706 align:middle
that you have some
critics on Wall Street.

54:41.036 --> 54:44.176 align:middle
And I would like to paraphrase
a couple of their criticisms

54:44.176 --> 54:45.886 align:middle
and get your reaction to them.

54:45.886 --> 54:50.926 align:middle
One is that the new policy
framework is that you react

54:50.926 --> 54:55.156 align:middle
to actual data and do
not react to forecasts,

54:55.156 --> 54:59.636 align:middle
yet the actual inflation data is
coming in hot and you're relying

54:59.636 --> 55:01.986 align:middle
on the forecast that
it will cool

55:01.986 --> 55:03.966 align:middle
down in order to make policy.

55:03.966 --> 55:06.726 align:middle
I wanted to get your view
on how you square that.

55:06.726 --> 55:09.666 align:middle
Another is that you have a
long runway, you've said,

55:09.666 --> 55:12.456 align:middle
for tapering with announcements.

55:12.456 --> 55:15.226 align:middle
But if the data keep coming
in faster than expected,

55:15.226 --> 55:19.046 align:middle
are you trapped by
fear of a taper tantrum

55:19.046 --> 55:24.216 align:middle
from advancing the time period
in which you announce a taper?

55:24.476 --> 55:27.016 align:middle
And, finally, you've
said the Fed knows how

55:27.016 --> 55:30.826 align:middle
to combat inflation, but raising
rates also slows the economy.

55:31.286 --> 55:34.816 align:middle
And there's a concern that you
might be sacrificing the economy

55:34.816 --> 55:38.226 align:middle
if you wait too long and have
to raise rates too quickly.

55:38.276 --> 55:40.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:40.306 --> 55:42.026 align:middle
So that's a-that's a
few questions there.

55:42.166 --> 55:49.926 align:middle
So let me say, first, I
think people misinterpret

55:49.926 --> 55:50.516 align:middle
the framework.

55:50.516 --> 55:52.506 align:middle
I think the-there's nothing
wrong with the framework,

55:52.506 --> 55:55.506 align:middle
and there's nothing in the
framework that would in any way,

55:55.576 --> 55:57.646 align:middle
you know, interfere
with our ability

55:57.646 --> 56:00.456 align:middle
to pursue our, our goals.

56:00.586 --> 56:02.466 align:middle
That's for starters.

56:02.946 --> 56:04.736 align:middle
All of our discussions
and all of our thinking

56:04.736 --> 56:05.826 align:middle
and planning are taking place

56:05.826 --> 56:07.896 align:middle
in the context of
our new framework.

56:07.896 --> 56:10.376 align:middle
We're strongly committed to
it, we think it's well suited

56:10.376 --> 56:14.506 align:middle
to our goals, including in
this-in this unique time.

56:14.576 --> 56:18.056 align:middle
And I think if you look at
the-look at the forecasts

56:18.186 --> 56:20.976 align:middle
that we've written
down, you know,

56:20.976 --> 56:22.686 align:middle
our Committee is
solidly behind them.

56:22.686 --> 56:25.166 align:middle
The forecasts are all
consistent with that.

56:26.056 --> 56:29.566 align:middle
You know, your specific
question, I guess, was,

56:29.676 --> 56:32.206 align:middle
will we be behind the curve?

56:32.786 --> 56:34.056 align:middle
And, you know, that's,

56:34.056 --> 56:36.226 align:middle
that's not the situation
we're facing at all.

56:36.226 --> 56:39.146 align:middle
The situation that
we, we addressed

56:39.146 --> 56:41.656 align:middle
in our-in our Statement
on Longer-Run Goals

56:41.656 --> 56:43.916 align:middle
and Monetary Policy
Strategy was a situation

56:43.916 --> 56:48.076 align:middle
in which employment was
at very high levels,

56:48.216 --> 56:49.386 align:middle
but inflation was low.

56:49.386 --> 56:50.226 align:middle
And what we said was,

56:50.226 --> 56:52.956 align:middle
we wouldn't raise
interest rates just

56:52.956 --> 56:55.516 align:middle
because unemployment was
low and employment was high

56:55.846 --> 56:57.476 align:middle
if there was no evidence
of inflation

56:57.476 --> 56:59.036 align:middle
or other troubling imbalances.

56:59.806 --> 57:00.936 align:middle
So that's what we said.

57:01.096 --> 57:02.836 align:middle
That is not at all
the current situation.

57:02.836 --> 57:05.596 align:middle
In the current situation, we
have many millions of people

57:05.596 --> 57:08.336 align:middle
who are unemployed, and we
have inflation running well

57:08.336 --> 57:09.776 align:middle
above our target.

57:10.146 --> 57:12.976 align:middle
The question we face with
this inflation has nothing

57:12.976 --> 57:13.986 align:middle
to do with our framework.

57:13.986 --> 57:18.096 align:middle
It's a very different,
very difficult version

57:18.096 --> 57:21.876 align:middle
of a standard investment-sorry,
a central banking question.

57:21.876 --> 57:23.496 align:middle
And that is, how do you separate

57:23.766 --> 57:27.356 align:middle
in inflation-how do you
separate things that,

57:27.426 --> 57:32.146 align:middle
that follow from broad upward
price pressures from things

57:32.146 --> 57:37.216 align:middle
that really are a function of,
of sort of idiosyncratic factors

57:37.216 --> 57:39.886 align:middle
in a particular-due
to particular things?

57:39.886 --> 57:42.356 align:middle
I mean, a classic example
was, to pick a narrow example,

57:42.356 --> 57:45.666 align:middle
was the cellphone
price war back in 2017.

57:45.666 --> 57:48.516 align:middle
If you remember, there
was-prices were incredibly low,

57:48.516 --> 57:51.156 align:middle
and it held down core PCE
by three-tenths or something

57:51.156 --> 57:52.406 align:middle
for a year, and then
it fell out.

57:52.746 --> 57:53.916 align:middle
So this is much bigger
than that.

57:54.226 --> 57:56.886 align:middle
And, of course, it's not-it's
not easy to tell in real time

57:56.886 --> 57:57.916 align:middle
which is which, but that's,

57:57.966 --> 57:59.856 align:middle
that's the question
you would face under,

58:00.306 --> 58:02.286 align:middle
really, any framework.

58:02.646 --> 58:04.266 align:middle
And, you know, we're
trying to sort that out.

58:04.266 --> 58:06.246 align:middle
I've tried to, to
explain that today

58:06.246 --> 58:07.636 align:middle
about how we think about that.

58:08.236 --> 58:13.136 align:middle
And, you know, we do think that
these are temporary factors,

58:13.136 --> 58:14.046 align:middle
and that they'll wane.

58:14.386 --> 58:16.466 align:middle
We can't be absolutely certain
about the timing of that,

58:16.466 --> 58:18.546 align:middle
and we're prepared to use
our tools as appropriate.

58:18.996 --> 58:20.986 align:middle
Your second one was?

58:20.986 --> 58:30.396 align:middle
Oh, you know, we will-we
will taper when we feel

58:30.396 --> 58:32.936 align:middle
that the economy has achieved
substantial further progress.

58:33.296 --> 58:37.266 align:middle
And we will communicate very
carefully in advance on that.

58:37.456 --> 58:38.546 align:middle
And that's what we're doing.

58:38.706 --> 58:40.126 align:middle
That's what we're
going to do, and,

58:40.126 --> 58:41.456 align:middle
and we will follow
through on that.

58:41.516 --> 58:44.546 align:middle
There's no-I mean, we
will do what we can

58:44.546 --> 58:45.846 align:middle
to avoid a market reaction.

58:45.846 --> 58:48.266 align:middle
But, ultimately, when we
achieve our macroeconomic goal,

58:48.266 --> 58:50.886 align:middle
we will-we will taper
as appropriate.

58:51.646 --> 58:56.446 align:middle
The third thing was-what
was the third thing?

58:56.726 --> 58:57.136 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

58:57.136 --> 58:59.796 align:middle
If you raise rates
to control inflation,

58:59.796 --> 59:01.426 align:middle
you also slow the economy.

59:01.426 --> 59:05.976 align:middle
And the history of the Fed is
that sometimes you go too far.

59:06.366 --> 59:06.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

59:07.396 --> 59:07.606 align:middle
That's right.

59:07.706 --> 59:13.786 align:middle
And we-look, we have to
balance the two-the two goals:

59:13.786 --> 59:15.306 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

59:15.306 --> 59:17.776 align:middle
Often they are-they do pull in
the same direction, of course.

59:18.616 --> 59:21.616 align:middle
But when we-when we
raise interest rates

59:21.616 --> 59:23.206 align:middle
to control inflation,
there's no question

59:23.206 --> 59:24.446 align:middle
that has an effect on activity.

59:24.446 --> 59:25.956 align:middle
And that's the channel-one
of the channels

59:25.956 --> 59:27.546 align:middle
through which we
get to inflation.

59:27.546 --> 59:31.476 align:middle
We don't think that we're in a
situation like that right now.

59:31.476 --> 59:33.096 align:middle
We think that the
economy is recovering

59:33.096 --> 59:37.746 align:middle
from a deep hole-an unusual
hole, actually, because it's

59:37.846 --> 59:40.016 align:middle
to do with, with
shutting down the economy.

59:40.456 --> 59:43.186 align:middle
It turns out it's a heck of
a lot easier to create demand

59:43.186 --> 59:46.236 align:middle
than it is to, you know, to
bring supply back up to snuff.

59:46.776 --> 59:48.456 align:middle
That's happening
all over the world.

59:48.726 --> 59:49.606 align:middle
There's no reason to think

59:49.606 --> 59:51.846 align:middle
that that process will
last indefinitely.

59:52.606 --> 59:55.326 align:middle
But we're going, you know,
we're going to watch carefully

59:55.326 --> 59:59.086 align:middle
to make sure that, that evolving
inflation and our understanding

59:59.086 --> 01:00:01.836 align:middle
of what's happening
is, is, is right.

01:00:02.236 --> 01:00:04.726 align:middle
And in the meantime, we'll
conduct policy appropriately.

01:00:05.166 --> 01:00:06.186 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

01:00:08.286 --> 01:00:09.076 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chair.

